/* PostgreSQL Mastery — Interview bank part 3
   Sections: performance (76 more, continuing interview-2.js) and scenario (100).
   Target: PostgreSQL 16–18. Extensions and third-party tools are labelled as such. */
PGM.INTERVIEW = PGM.INTERVIEW || {};

PGM.INTERVIEW.performance = (PGM.INTERVIEW.performance || []).concat([
{ q: `What is pg_stat_statements and how do you use it to find the queries worth optimizing?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_stat_statements</code> is a contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> (shipped with PostgreSQL but not enabled by default). It normalizes statements (literals become <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>) and aggregates calls, total/mean execution time, rows, buffer hits/reads, temp blocks and WAL bytes per query shape. Enable it with <code>shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_stat_statements'</code>, restart, then <code>CREATE EXTENSION pg_stat_statements;</code>.</p><pre>SELECT queryid, calls,
       round(total_exec_time::numeric, 0)  AS total_ms,
       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2)   AS mean_ms,
       rows, shared_blks_read, temp_blks_written,
       left(query, 80) AS query
FROM pg_stat_statements
ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC
LIMIT 20;</pre><p>Sort by <code>total_exec_time</code> to find what costs the server the most, by <code>mean_exec_time</code> for user-facing latency, and by <code>shared_blks_read</code> or <code>temp_blks_written</code> for I/O and spill problems. Reset with <code>pg_stat_statements_reset()</code> before a benchmark window.</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','pg_stat_statements'] },
{ q: `A query takes 2 ms but runs 5 million times a day; another takes 30 s and runs twice. Which do you optimize first?`,
  a: `<p>Usually the 2 ms one: 2 ms × 5M = 10,000 s (~2.8 hours) of server time per day versus 60 s for the report. <code>total_exec_time</code> in <code>pg_stat_statements</code> captures exactly this. Shaving 1 ms off the hot query (for example an index-only scan instead of a heap lookup, or removing an N+1 pattern) frees more CPU than making the report instant.</p><p>Exceptions: the slow query may hold locks, blow out the cache, create temp files or violate an SLA — then latency matters more than aggregate time. A good rule is to look at both lists: top by total time (capacity) and top by mean/max time (user experience).</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','methodology'] },
{ q: `Why can the same query be slow on the first run and fast on the second? How do you benchmark fairly?`,
  a: `<p>The first run reads pages from disk (or the OS cache) into <code>shared_buffers</code>; later runs hit the cache. In <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> this shows as <code>shared read=</code> on the cold run and <code>shared hit=</code> on the warm run. Plan caching in the session, catalog caches and JIT compilation also make first runs slower.</p><p>To benchmark fairly: run the query several times and report the median, state whether you measure cold or warm cache, compare <strong>buffers</strong> rather than only milliseconds (buffer counts are stable across runs and machines), and test with production-like data volume and parameter values. Use <code>pgbench</code> with a custom script for concurrency effects.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','benchmarking'] },
{ q: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows "Planning Time: 450 ms" and "Execution Time: 3 ms". What causes high planning time?`,
  a: `<p>Common causes:</p><ul><li><strong>Many partitions</strong>: the planner must consider every partition unless it can prune at plan time; thousands of partitions with run-time-only pruning is expensive.</li><li><strong>Many joined tables</strong>: join-order search grows factorially; above <code>join_collapse_limit</code>/<code>from_collapse_limit</code> (8) it stops reordering, above <code>geqo_threshold</code> (12) the genetic optimizer kicks in.</li><li>Many indexes per table, large IN-lists, complex views expanded inline, or extended statistics on many columns.</li></ul><p>Fixes: use prepared statements so the plan is reused (generic plans), reduce partition count or ensure plan-time pruning with literal partition keys, simplify views, and check <code>pg_stat_statements.mean_plan_time</code> (requires <code>pg_stat_statements.track_planning = on</code>).</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','explain'] },
{ q: `What do join_collapse_limit, from_collapse_limit and geqo_threshold control?`,
  a: `<p><code>from_collapse_limit</code> (default 8) controls whether subqueries in FROM are flattened into the parent's join list; <code>join_collapse_limit</code> (8) controls whether explicit <code>JOIN</code> syntax is reordered freely. When the number of relations exceeds the limit, the planner keeps the written order for the remainder, which bounds planning time but may lock in a bad order. <code>geqo_threshold</code> (12) switches to the Genetic Query Optimizer, a randomized heuristic search; plans may then vary between runs.</p><p>For a 15-way reporting join you can raise the limits for that session (<code>SET join_collapse_limit = 16</code>) if planning time is acceptable, or set <code>join_collapse_limit = 1</code> to force the written order when you know the best order better than the planner.</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','gucs'] },
{ q: `Explain generic vs custom plans for prepared statements and plan_cache_mode.`,
  a: `<p>A prepared statement (server-side <code>PREPARE</code>, or the extended protocol used by most drivers) is planned with the actual parameter values for the first 5 executions (<strong>custom plans</strong>). After that, PostgreSQL builds a <strong>generic plan</strong> that ignores the values and switches to it if its estimated cost is not much worse than the average custom plan. This saves planning time but can be disastrous for skewed data: <code>status = $1</code> might be 0.1% for 'pending' and 80% for 'delivered'.</p><pre>SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan;   -- or force_generic_plan / auto (default)
EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = $1;  -- PG 16+</pre><p>Symptom: a query that is fast in psql (literals) but slow from the application after the sixth call. Fix per role/database with <code>ALTER ROLE app SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code> or per statement in the application.</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','prepared-statements'] },
{ q: `What are extended statistics and when do you need CREATE STATISTICS?`,
  a: `<p>The planner assumes columns are independent and multiplies selectivities. For correlated columns (<code>city</code> and <code>country</code>, <code>model</code> and <code>brand</code>) this underestimates row counts badly — e.g. <code>city='Mumbai' AND country='India'</code> estimated as 1% × 20% when the true answer is just 1%. Extended statistics fix this:</p><pre>CREATE STATISTICS customers_geo (dependencies, ndistinct, mcv)
  ON country, city FROM customers;
ANALYZE customers;</pre><ul><li><code>dependencies</code>: functional dependency degree between columns (for equality filters).</li><li><code>ndistinct</code>: number of distinct combinations (improves GROUP BY estimates).</li><li><code>mcv</code>: most common value combinations (handles skewed combinations and some range/IN conditions).</li></ul><p>Since PG 14 you can also create statistics on expressions. Check results in <code>pg_stats_ext</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','planner'] },
{ q: `How do default_statistics_target and ALTER COLUMN ... SET STATISTICS affect plans?`,
  a: `<p><code>default_statistics_target</code> (100) sets how many most-common values and histogram buckets ANALYZE keeps per column; the sample size is 300 × target rows. For a large table with a skewed column (a <code>tenant_id</code> with thousands of tenants, a few huge), 100 MCVs may miss important values, causing bad estimates for mid-size tenants.</p><pre>ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN customer_id SET STATISTICS 1000;
ANALYZE orders;</pre><p>Raise it per column rather than globally: higher targets make ANALYZE slower and planning slightly slower for every query touching the column. Maximum is 10000.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics'] },
{ q: `What is the "correlation" statistic and why does it matter for index scans?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_stats.correlation</code> (-1 to 1) measures how well the physical row order matches the logical order of a column. An append-only <code>created_at</code> column is near 1: rows with nearby timestamps live on nearby pages, so a range index scan touches few pages mostly sequentially. A random UUID column is near 0: each index entry points to a different page, so a range scan becomes random I/O.</p><p>The planner uses correlation to interpolate between best-case and worst-case page fetch cost, which is why it may pick a sequential or bitmap scan for a low-correlation column even when selectivity looks good. <code>CLUSTER</code> can physically reorder a table once (with an exclusive lock), and time-ordered keys (UUIDv7 via <code>uuidv7()</code> in PG 18, or bigint identities) keep correlation high naturally.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','indexes'] },
{ q: `Which columns of pg_stats do you look at when diagnosing a bad estimate?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT attname, null_frac, n_distinct, most_common_vals,
       most_common_freqs, histogram_bounds, correlation
FROM pg_stats
WHERE tablename = 'orders' AND attname = 'status';</pre><ul><li><code>null_frac</code>: fraction of NULLs (bad if your filter is <code>IS NULL</code> and this is stale).</li><li><code>n_distinct</code>: positive = absolute count, negative = fraction of rows (-1 means unique). Badly underestimated on huge tables; override with <code>ALTER COLUMN ... SET (n_distinct = ...)</code>.</li><li><code>most_common_vals/freqs</code>: exact frequencies for skewed values.</li><li><code>histogram_bounds</code>: equal-population buckets for the remaining values, used for ranges.</li></ul><p>Compare these with the real distribution (<code>SELECT status, count(*) ... GROUP BY 1</code>). If they disagree, run ANALYZE, raise the statistics target, or add extended statistics.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','pg_stats'] },
{ q: `When must you run ANALYZE manually instead of trusting autovacuum?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Right after a bulk load</strong> or large delete inside a job: queries in the same job run before autovacuum notices (it waits for <code>autovacuum_naptime</code> and the threshold).</li><li><strong>Temporary tables</strong>: autovacuum never processes them; a temp table with 1M rows is assumed to be tiny without ANALYZE.</li><li><strong>Partitioned parents</strong>: autovacuum analyzes leaf partitions but not the partitioned table itself, so statistics for queries spanning partitions come only from a manual <code>ANALYZE parent</code>.</li><li>After <code>CREATE STATISTICS</code>, after creating an expression index, and after a major-version upgrade (before PG 18, <code>pg_upgrade</code> did not carry optimizer statistics; PG 18 carries most but not extended statistics).</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['statistics','vacuum'] },
{ q: `Queries for "today's orders" get bad estimates even though ANALYZE ran last night. Why?`,
  a: `<p>Rows inserted since the last ANALYZE lie beyond the histogram's upper bound, so a naive estimate would be near zero. PostgreSQL mitigates this: when a B-tree index exists on the column, the planner probes the index for the actual current minimum/maximum (<code>get_actual_variable_range</code>) and extends the histogram. Without such an index, or for correlated conditions (<code>status = 'pending' AND order_date &gt; now() - interval '1 hour'</code>), estimates can still be off by orders of magnitude, leading to nested loops over thousands of rows.</p><p>Fixes: index the timestamp column, lower <code>autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor</code> for the table so it is analyzed more often, ANALYZE after large batch inserts, and use extended statistics for correlated predicates.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','planner'] },
{ q: `How is work_mem actually allocated, and why can a modest value still cause OOM?`,
  a: `<p><code>work_mem</code> (default 4MB) is the limit <strong>per sort or hash operation, per process</strong> — not per connection or per query. A single query with 3 hash joins and 2 sorts can use 5 × work_mem, hash nodes may use <code>work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier</code> (default 2.0), and each parallel worker gets its own allowance.</p><p>Worst case: 300 connections × 5 nodes × 64MB × 2 = 192GB. That is why the global default stays small (8–64MB on most OLTP servers) and big values are set only where needed:</p><pre>SET LOCAL work_mem = '512MB';   -- inside the reporting transaction
ALTER ROLE reporting SET work_mem = '256MB';</pre><p>Check spills with <code>log_temp_files = 0</code> and <code>temp_blks_written</code> in <code>pg_stat_statements</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','work_mem'] },
{ q: `What is hash_mem_multiplier and why was it introduced?`,
  a: `<p>Added in PG 13 (default 1.0, raised to 2.0 in PG 15). Hash-based nodes (Hash Join, Hash Aggregate, Memoize) may use <code>work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier</code>. Before PG 13 hash aggregation could ignore work_mem entirely; when it was made to spill to disk, some workloads regressed, so this multiplier lets hash operations (which degrade badly when spilling to many batches) get more memory than sorts (which degrade gracefully). Raise it when you see <code>Batches: 8+</code> on hash joins without wanting to raise work_mem for sorts too.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','work_mem'] },
{ q: `What are maintenance_work_mem and autovacuum_work_mem used for, and how should you size them?`,
  a: `<p><code>maintenance_work_mem</code> (default 64MB) is used by <code>CREATE INDEX</code>, <code>VACUUM</code>, <code>ALTER TABLE ADD FOREIGN KEY</code> and <code>REINDEX</code>. Larger values speed up index builds (fewer sort runs) and let VACUUM remember more dead tuple IDs per pass, so it scans each index fewer times.</p><p><code>autovacuum_work_mem</code> (default -1 = use maintenance_work_mem) applies per autovacuum worker, so with 3–6 workers keep it moderate (256MB–1GB). Before PG 17 VACUUM could use at most 1GB for dead TIDs; PG 17's TidStore removed that cap and uses memory far more efficiently. For a one-off big index build: <code>SET maintenance_work_mem = '2GB'</code> in that session, plus <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code> for parallel B-tree builds.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','vacuum','indexes'] },
{ q: `How do you size shared_buffers and why not give it 80% of RAM?`,
  a: `<p>The common starting point is ~25% of RAM on a dedicated server (e.g. 16GB on 64GB). PostgreSQL relies on the OS page cache as a second level, so data read from disk is often cached twice (<strong>double buffering</strong>). Very large shared_buffers also means bigger checkpoints (more dirty pages to flush), more memory for the buffer mapping table, and less RAM for <code>work_mem</code>, maintenance and the OS cache.</p><p>Tune by measurement: if the hot working set (frequently used tables plus indexes) fits in 40% of RAM, a larger setting can help; check with the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pg_buffercache</code> and <code>pg_stat_io</code>. Use huge pages for shared_buffers above a few GB. Changing it requires a restart.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','shared_buffers'] },
{ q: `effective_cache_size allocates no memory — so how can changing it alter query plans?`,
  a: `<p>Nothing is allocated — it is only a <strong>planner hint</strong>: an estimate of how much memory (shared_buffers + OS page cache) is available to cache data for one query. A higher value makes index scans look cheaper because repeated page fetches are assumed to be cached. Typical setting: 50–75% of RAM. Setting it too low on a big server pushes the planner towards sequential scans and hash joins; it is safe to change without restart.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','planner'] },
{ q: `How do you compute the buffer cache hit ratio and why can it be misleading?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT datname,
       round(100.0 * blks_hit / nullif(blks_hit + blks_read, 0), 2) AS hit_pct
FROM pg_stat_database
WHERE datname = current_database();</pre><p>A healthy OLTP system is often above 99%. But the ratio is misleading: <code>blks_read</code> counts reads from outside shared_buffers, which may still be served from the OS page cache (fast). A 99.9% hit ratio can hide one query doing a huge sequential scan, and analytic workloads legitimately have lower ratios. Prefer per-query numbers (<code>shared_blks_read</code> in <code>pg_stat_statements</code>), per-table numbers (<code>pg_statio_user_tables</code>) and real I/O latency (<code>pg_stat_io</code> with <code>track_io_timing = on</code>).</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','memory'] },
{ q: `How can you see which relations occupy shared_buffers?`,
  a: `<p>With the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pg_buffercache</code> (contrib):</p><pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_buffercache;
SELECT c.relname,
       count(*) AS buffers,
       pg_size_pretty(count(*) * 8192) AS cached,
       round(100.0 * count(*) FILTER (WHERE b.isdirty) / count(*), 1) AS dirty_pct
FROM pg_buffercache b
JOIN pg_class c ON b.relfilenode = pg_relation_filenode(c.oid)
WHERE b.reldatabase = (SELECT oid FROM pg_database WHERE datname = current_database())
GROUP BY c.relname
ORDER BY buffers DESC
LIMIT 10;</pre><p>Useful to confirm that a hot index really stays cached, or that a nightly report evicted the OLTP working set. Reading the whole view takes buffer header locks briefly, so do not poll it every second on a huge cache; <code>pg_buffercache_summary()</code> (PG 16+) is cheaper.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','monitoring','extension'] },
{ q: `What is pg_prewarm and when would you use it?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_prewarm</code> is a contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> that loads a relation into shared_buffers or the OS cache: <code>SELECT pg_prewarm('orders_pkey');</code>. With <code>shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_prewarm'</code> its autoprewarm worker periodically saves the list of cached blocks and reloads them after a restart. Use it after a failover or restart so the first minutes of traffic are not hitting cold disks, or before a benchmark. It does not help if the working set is larger than memory.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','extension'] },
{ q: `What happens during a checkpoint and which settings control it?`,
  a: `<p>A checkpoint writes all dirty pages from shared_buffers to the data files, fsyncs them and writes a checkpoint record to WAL, so crash recovery only needs to replay WAL from that point. Triggers: <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (default 5min) elapses, or WAL since the last checkpoint approaches <code>max_wal_size</code> (default 1GB).</p><p><code>checkpoint_completion_target</code> (default 0.9) spreads the writes over 90% of the interval to avoid I/O spikes. Typical production tuning: <code>checkpoint_timeout = 15min</code>, <code>max_wal_size</code> = 16–64GB so most checkpoints are time-driven. Trade-off: longer intervals mean longer crash recovery and more WAL on disk, but fewer full-page writes and smoother I/O.</p>`,
  tags: ['checkpoints','wal','io'] },
{ q: `The log says "checkpoints are occurring too frequently". What does it mean and how do you fix it?`,
  a: `<p>Checkpoints are being forced by WAL volume reaching <code>max_wal_size</code> before <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (the warning fires when they are closer than <code>checkpoint_warning</code>, 30 s). Frequent checkpoints cause I/O bursts and multiply WAL volume because the first change to each page after a checkpoint writes a full page image.</p><pre>-- PG 17+: pg_stat_checkpointer (earlier: pg_stat_bgwriter)
SELECT num_timed, num_requested, write_time, sync_time
FROM pg_stat_checkpointer;</pre><p>If <code>num_requested</code> is a large share, raise <code>max_wal_size</code> (it is a soft limit, reloadable) so checkpoints become timed, and make sure the disk has room for that much WAL. Also look at what generates so much WAL (bulk updates, index-heavy tables, <code>pg_stat_statements.wal_bytes</code>).</p>`,
  tags: ['checkpoints','wal'] },
{ q: `What are full-page writes and how do they affect WAL volume?`,
  a: `<p>With <code>full_page_writes = on</code> (required for safety unless the storage guarantees atomic 8KB writes), the first modification of a page after each checkpoint logs the entire 8KB page into WAL to protect against torn pages. A tiny update right after a checkpoint can therefore produce 8KB of WAL instead of ~100 bytes, which is why WAL volume spikes after each checkpoint and why longer checkpoint intervals reduce WAL.</p><p>Mitigations: increase <code>checkpoint_timeout</code>/<code>max_wal_size</code>, enable <code>wal_compression</code> (<code>lz4</code> or <code>zstd</code> in PG 15+) to compress full-page images, and avoid needless updates (e.g. updating rows with unchanged values).</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','checkpoints'] },
{ q: `What does synchronous_commit = off do and when is it acceptable?`,
  a: `<p>With <code>off</code>, COMMIT returns before the WAL is flushed to disk; the WAL writer flushes within about 3 × <code>wal_writer_delay</code> (default 200ms). A crash can lose the last fraction of a second of committed transactions, but <strong>never corrupts</strong> the database — it is consistent, just slightly behind. Commit latency drops from an fsync (0.1–10ms) to microseconds.</p><p>It can be set per transaction: <code>SET LOCAL synchronous_commit = off</code> for click-tracking, metrics or session data, while payments keep the default. With replicas, the values <code>remote_write</code>, <code>on</code> and <code>remote_apply</code> control how far the standby must have progressed before commit returns.</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','durability'] },
{ q: `What is pg_stat_io (PG 16+) and what problems does it reveal?`,
  a: `<p>A core view that breaks I/O down by backend type (client backend, autovacuum, checkpointer, background writer...), object (relation, temp relation) and context (normal, vacuum, bulkread, bulkwrite):</p><pre>SELECT backend_type, object, context, reads, writes, extends, fsyncs, evictions
FROM pg_stat_io
WHERE reads &gt; 0 OR writes &gt; 0
ORDER BY writes DESC NULLS LAST;</pre><p>Key signal: many <code>writes</code> by <strong>client backends</strong> in the normal context means backends must flush dirty pages themselves before they can reuse a buffer — shared_buffers is too small or the background writer/checkpointer cannot keep up. With <code>track_io_timing = on</code> you also get read/write times. PG 18 adds byte counters and WAL I/O rows.</p>`,
  tags: ['io','monitoring'] },
{ q: `How do you measure table bloat accurately?`,
  a: `<p>Exact measurement uses the contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pgstattuple</code>, which scans the table:</p><pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgstattuple;
SELECT table_len, tuple_percent, dead_tuple_percent, free_percent
FROM pgstattuple('orders');
-- cheaper, sampling-based:
SELECT * FROM pgstattuple_approx('orders');</pre><p>For a quick fleet-wide view, check <code>n_dead_tup</code> and <code>last_autovacuum</code> in <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code>, or use the well-known estimation queries that compare actual pages with pages expected from <code>pg_stats.avg_width</code>. Remember that free space is not always bad: after VACUUM, free space is reused by future inserts. It becomes a problem when it is large, persistent and in hot tables, making scans read many mostly-empty pages.</p>`,
  tags: ['bloat','vacuum'] },
{ q: `How does index bloat happen and how do you fix it without downtime?`,
  a: `<p>B-tree pages split when full but are only reclaimed when completely empty, so update/delete-heavy patterns (e.g. a queue where old keys are deleted and new keys always appended) leave many half-empty pages. Non-HOT updates also add new index entries for every index. PG 13+ deduplication and PG 14+ bottom-up deletion reduce this a lot, but old indexes can still be 2–5× their ideal size.</p><pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgstattuple;
SELECT avg_leaf_density, leaf_fragmentation FROM pgstatindex('orders_pkey');
REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_pkey;   -- PG 12+, no blocking of reads/writes</pre><p><code>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> builds a new index alongside the old one and swaps them; it needs extra disk, takes longer, and on failure leaves an <code>INVALID</code> index (suffix <code>_ccnew</code>) that you must drop.</p>`,
  tags: ['bloat','indexes'] },
{ q: `What are HOT updates and how does fillfactor help them?`,
  a: `<p>A Heap-Only Tuple (HOT) update happens when (1) no indexed column changes and (2) the new tuple version fits on the same heap page. Then no index entries are added — the old tuple points to the new one in a chain — and page pruning can clean old versions without VACUUM. This greatly reduces write amplification and index bloat.</p><pre>ALTER TABLE accounts SET (fillfactor = 80);  -- leave 20% free per page
SELECT relname, n_tup_upd, n_tup_hot_upd,
       round(100.0 * n_tup_hot_upd / nullif(n_tup_upd, 0), 1) AS hot_pct
FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_tup_upd DESC LIMIT 10;</pre><p>Fillfactor applies to newly written pages (rewrite with <code>VACUUM FULL</code>/<code>pg_repack</code> to apply everywhere). Also avoid indexing frequently updated columns like <code>updated_at</code> or <code>last_seen</code> unless needed — one such index disables HOT for every update.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','hot','bloat'] },
{ q: `How do you find unused indexes, and what must you check before dropping one?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT s.schemaname, s.relname, s.indexrelname, s.idx_scan,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid)) AS size
FROM pg_stat_user_indexes s
JOIN pg_index i ON i.indexrelid = s.indexrelid
WHERE s.idx_scan = 0
  AND NOT i.indisunique AND NOT i.indisprimary
ORDER BY pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid) DESC;</pre><p>Before dropping: check how long statistics have been accumulating (<code>pg_stat_database.stats_reset</code>) — a month-end report may use the index only once a month; check <strong>every replica</strong>, because replica reads are counted only on the replica; keep indexes backing constraints; and drop with <code>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>. PG 16+ also shows <code>last_idx_scan</code>, which tells you when it was last used.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','monitoring'] },
{ q: `What are redundant or duplicate indexes and how do you spot them?`,
  a: `<p>An index on <code>(customer_id)</code> is redundant when an index on <code>(customer_id, order_date)</code> exists — the composite index serves the same lookups (slightly larger, but one fewer index to maintain). Exact duplicates (same columns, same predicate) come from ORMs and repeated migrations.</p><pre>SELECT indrelid::regclass AS table_name,
       array_agg(indexrelid::regclass) AS indexes
FROM pg_index
GROUP BY indrelid, indkey::text, coalesce(indpred::text, ''), coalesce(indexprs::text, '')
HAVING count(*) &gt; 1;</pre><p>Be careful with prefix redundancy: keep the single-column index if it is unique, much smaller and very hot, or if the composite index's extra columns make it far larger.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes'] },
{ q: `What does each additional index cost on a write-heavy table?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Every INSERT (and every non-HOT UPDATE) inserts into <strong>every</strong> index: more CPU, more random I/O, more WAL (often index WAL exceeds heap WAL).</li><li>Any indexed column changing disables HOT for that update.</li><li>More pages competing for shared_buffers, longer VACUUM (each index is scanned), larger backups and replicas.</li><li>More planning work.</li></ul><p>A table with 15 indexes can easily insert 5× slower than with 3. Treat indexes as a budget: each must justify itself by a query in <code>pg_stat_statements</code>, and prefer one well-designed composite or covering index over several single-column ones.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','writes'] },
{ q: `What is the N+1 query problem and how do you detect and fix it?`,
  a: `<p>The application runs one query to fetch N parents, then one query per parent to fetch children — 1 + N round trips. Each query is fast (0.2 ms), so nothing appears in slow-query logs, but 500 rows × (0.2 ms + 0.5 ms network) adds 350 ms per page view plus heavy load from millions of tiny queries.</p><p>Detection: <code>pg_stat_statements</code> shows a simple PK lookup with an enormous <code>calls</code> count; APM traces show hundreds of identical spans per request. Fixes:</p><pre>-- one round trip with a join
SELECT c.id, c.name, o.id AS order_id, o.total_amount
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id
WHERE c.country = 'India';
-- or a batched second query
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ANY($1::int[]);</pre><p>In ORMs, use eager loading (<code>Include</code> in EF Core, <code>select_related/prefetch_related</code> in Django, <code>joinedload</code> in SQLAlchemy). And index the foreign key used by the batched query.</p>`,
  tags: ['n+1','application'] },
{ q: `Why is OFFSET pagination slow on deep pages, and what is the alternative?`,
  a: `<p><code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000</code> must produce and discard 100,000 rows before returning 20 — cost grows linearly with page depth, and results shift when rows are inserted between requests. <strong>Keyset (seek) pagination</strong> remembers the last row seen and asks for rows after it:</p><pre>CREATE INDEX orders_date_id ON orders (order_date DESC, id DESC);
SELECT id, order_date, total_amount
FROM orders
WHERE (order_date, id) &lt; ($1, $2)       -- values from the last row of the previous page
ORDER BY order_date DESC, id DESC
LIMIT 20;</pre><p>Each page is an index range scan of 20 entries, constant time at any depth. Trade-off: no "jump to page 873"; you offer next/previous and filters instead, which is what infinite-scroll and APIs need anyway.</p>`,
  tags: ['pagination','indexes'] },
{ q: `How do you implement keyset pagination correctly when the sort column is not unique?`,
  a: `<p>Add a unique tiebreaker (the primary key) to both the ORDER BY and the seek predicate, and use a <strong>row-value comparison</strong> so a single composite index can serve it:</p><pre>WHERE (order_date, id) &lt; ('2025-06-01 10:00', 48211)
ORDER BY order_date DESC, id DESC
LIMIT 20</pre><p>Writing it as <code>order_date &lt; $1 OR (order_date = $1 AND id &lt; $2)</code> is logically equivalent but often not used efficiently as a single index range. All columns must sort in the same direction for the row comparison; for mixed directions (e.g. <code>price ASC, id DESC</code>) you need the expanded OR form or a negated expression index. NULLs in the sort column also need special handling (prefer NOT NULL columns).</p>`,
  tags: ['pagination','sql'] },
{ q: `Why is SELECT count(*) on a large table slow and what are the alternatives?`,
  a: `<p>Because of MVCC, PostgreSQL cannot keep a single row counter: different transactions see different row counts. <code>count(*)</code> must scan the table, or an index with an index-only scan if the visibility map is mostly set (parallel workers help). On a 500M-row table that is still seconds to minutes.</p><p>Alternatives:</p><ul><li>Estimate: <code>SELECT reltuples::bigint FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'orders'::regclass;</code> (updated by VACUUM/ANALYZE).</li><li>Estimate a filtered count by parsing the <code>rows=</code> of <code>EXPLAIN</code>.</li><li>Maintain a counter table via triggers or in the app (beware hot-row contention; use sharded counter rows).</li><li>Show "1,000+" instead of the exact total in UIs; run exact counts on a replica or in a materialized view.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['count','mvcc'] },
{ q: `How do you make LIKE '%term%' fast?`,
  a: `<p>A leading wildcard cannot use a B-tree. Use the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pg_trgm</code> (contrib) with a GIN (or GiST) trigram index:</p><pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX products_name_trgm ON products USING gin (name gin_trgm_ops);
SELECT id, name FROM products WHERE name ILIKE '%cable%';</pre><p>The index splits strings into 3-character grams; the search term must have at least 3 characters to be selective. It also accelerates <code>ILIKE</code>, regex (<code>~</code>) and similarity (<code>%</code>). For word-based search with ranking and stemming, use full-text search (<code>tsvector</code> + GIN) instead.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','pg_trgm','search'] },
{ q: `Why doesn't my B-tree index help LIKE 'abc%' and how do you fix it?`,
  a: `<p>Prefix searches can use a B-tree only if the index's collation sorts byte-wise. With a linguistic collation like <code>en_US.UTF-8</code>, the planner cannot turn <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> into a range. Options:</p><pre>CREATE INDEX customers_name_pattern ON customers (name text_pattern_ops);
-- or an index with the C collation
CREATE INDEX customers_name_c ON customers (name COLLATE "C");</pre><p>A <code>text_pattern_ops</code> index supports <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> and <code>~ '^abc'</code> but not ordinary <code>&lt;</code>/<code>ORDER BY</code> in the database collation, so you may need both indexes. Databases created with the C collation (or the builtin C.UTF-8 provider in PG 17+) do not need this.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','collation'] },
{ q: `A query with OR across two columns is slow. What are your options?`,
  a: `<p><code>WHERE customer_id = 42 OR email = 'x@y.com'</code> cannot use one composite index. PostgreSQL can combine two separate indexes with a <strong>BitmapOr</strong> if both columns are indexed. If it still chooses a sequential scan (estimates, or one side unindexed), rewrite as a UNION:</p><pre>SELECT * FROM customers WHERE id = 42
UNION
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE email = 'x@y.com';</pre><p>Use <code>UNION ALL</code> if duplicates are impossible or acceptable (cheaper). Also look for ORs generated by optional filters (<code>WHERE ($1 IS NULL OR col = $1)</code>), which defeat indexes — build the SQL dynamically with only the filters actually provided.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','indexes'] },
{ q: `IN with 10,000 literals vs = ANY(array) vs joining a VALUES list — which performs better?`,
  a: `<p>A huge literal IN-list is parsed and planned on every call (expensive planning, and each distinct length is a different statement in <code>pg_stat_statements</code>). <code>= ANY($1::bigint[])</code> sends one array parameter, keeps one prepared statement, and is executed as an index scan with an array key (PG 17 greatly improved B-tree handling of array keys). For very large lists, joining against <code>VALUES</code>, <code>unnest($1)</code> or a temp table lets the planner choose a hash join instead of thousands of index probes:</p><pre>SELECT o.*
FROM unnest($1::bigint[]) AS k(id)
JOIN orders o ON o.id = k.id;</pre><p>Rule: pass arrays, not literal lists; switch to a join (and ANALYZE a temp table) for tens of thousands of keys.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','application'] },
{ q: `NOT IN vs NOT EXISTS — why does it matter for both correctness and performance?`,
  a: `<p><code>NOT IN (subquery)</code> returns no rows if the subquery yields a single NULL (three-valued logic), and because of that the planner cannot turn it into an efficient anti-join; it becomes a hashed or (worse) plain SubPlan evaluated per row. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> has the intuitive semantics and is planned as a <strong>Hash Anti Join</strong> or Merge Anti Join:</p><pre>SELECT c.id FROM customers c
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);</pre><p>Rule: always use <code>NOT EXISTS</code> (or <code>LEFT JOIN ... WHERE o.id IS NULL</code>) for anti-joins.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','planner'] },
{ q: `What makes a predicate non-sargable (unable to use an index)? Give examples.`,
  a: `<p>A condition can use an index only when the indexed expression appears as-is on one side of an indexable operator. Common killers:</p><ul><li>Functions on the column: <code>WHERE date(order_date) = '2025-01-01'</code>, <code>WHERE lower(email) = ...</code> (without an expression index).</li><li>Arithmetic on the column: <code>WHERE salary * 12 &gt; 1000000</code>; rewrite as <code>salary &gt; 1000000 / 12.0</code>.</li><li>Type mismatches: comparing a <code>varchar</code> column to a <code>numeric</code> parameter casts the column; drivers sending <code>text</code> for a <code>uuid</code> column.</li><li>Leading wildcards and negations (<code>&lt;&gt;</code>, <code>NOT LIKE</code>).</li></ul><pre>-- sargable rewrite of date(order_date) = '2025-01-01'
WHERE order_date &gt;= '2025-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-02'</pre>`,
  tags: ['indexes','sql'] },
{ q: `jsonb_ops vs jsonb_path_ops GIN indexes — what is the difference?`,
  a: `<p>The default <code>jsonb_ops</code> indexes every key and value separately and supports <code>@&gt;</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>?|</code>, <code>?&amp;</code> and jsonpath operators. <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> indexes hashes of paths to values: it supports only containment (<code>@&gt;</code>) and jsonpath (<code>@?</code>, <code>@@</code>), but is typically 2–3× smaller and faster for containment queries.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX products_attr_path ON products USING gin (attributes jsonb_path_ops);
SELECT id FROM products WHERE attributes @&gt; '{"brand": "Acme"}';</pre><p>If you always filter one scalar key, a B-tree expression index on <code>(attributes-&gt;&gt;'brand')</code> is smaller still and supports ranges and sorting. GIN indexes also have a pending list (<code>fastupdate</code>) that can make some inserts slow when it is flushed.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','jsonb','gin'] },
{ q: `When is a BRIN index the right choice?`,
  a: `<p>BRIN (Block Range INdex) stores min/max per range of pages (default 128 pages). It is tiny (a few hundred KB for a 100GB table) and cheap to maintain, but only effective when values are strongly correlated with physical order — append-only time-series, logs, events with <code>created_at</code>.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX events_created_brin ON events USING brin (created_at)
  WITH (pages_per_range = 32);</pre><p>The query becomes a Bitmap Heap Scan reading only page ranges whose min/max overlaps the predicate (lossy, rechecked). Avoid it when rows are updated or inserted out of order (correlation drops), for point lookups, or for small tables. PG 14 added <code>minmax_multi</code> operator classes that tolerate some outliers.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','brin'] },
{ q: `Why does EXPLAIN show "Index Only Scan ... Heap Fetches: 48000"?`,
  a: `<p>An index-only scan still has to check visibility for each entry: if the heap page is marked all-visible in the <strong>visibility map</strong>, it skips the heap; otherwise it fetches the heap tuple. High Heap Fetches means many pages were modified since the last VACUUM, so the scan is effectively an ordinary index scan.</p><p>Fix by vacuuming more often so the visibility map stays current — especially on insert-heavy tables (PG 13+ insert-triggered autovacuum helps; lower <code>autovacuum_vacuum_insert_scale_factor</code> for big tables). Check with <code>pg_visibility</code> <span class="tag ext">extension</span> or by running <code>VACUUM</code> and comparing the plan.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','vacuum','explain'] },
{ q: `How do you diagnose lock contention in real time?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT a.pid, a.wait_event_type, a.wait_event, a.state,
       pg_blocking_pids(a.pid) AS blocked_by,
       now() - a.xact_start AS xact_age,
       left(a.query, 60) AS query
FROM pg_stat_activity a
WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(a.pid)) &gt; 0;</pre><p>Then look at the blockers (often <code>idle in transaction</code> sessions or a DDL waiting in the queue). Long term: set <code>log_lock_waits = on</code> (logs waits longer than <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, 1s), track <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code> counts over time, and check <code>pg_locks</code> for the lock modes involved. Fix patterns: shorter transactions, consistent lock ordering, <code>lock_timeout</code> for DDL, and avoiding hot rows.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','monitoring'] },
{ q: `Many sessions update the same counter row and throughput collapses. Why, and how do you fix it?`,
  a: `<p>Row-level locks serialize all updates of one row: each transaction must wait until the previous one commits. Throughput is capped at 1 / (transaction duration), and every update also creates a new tuple version, bloating that page.</p><p>Fixes:</p><ul><li>Shard the counter: N rows per counter (<code>slot = random 0..15</code>), sum them on read.</li><li>Append events (<code>INSERT</code> only) and aggregate periodically.</li><li>Keep the update as the last statement in a short transaction so the lock is held briefly.</li><li>Accumulate in the application or Redis and flush in batches.</li></ul><pre>UPDATE page_views SET hits = hits + 1
WHERE page_id = 42 AND slot = floor(random() * 16)::int;</pre>`,
  tags: ['locks','contention'] },
{ q: `How does SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED help job queues?`,
  a: `<p>Workers polling a queue with plain <code>FOR UPDATE</code> all block on the same first row. <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> makes each worker skip rows already locked by others, so N workers take N different jobs concurrently:</p><pre>WITH job AS (
  SELECT id FROM jobs
  WHERE status = 'queued'
  ORDER BY id
  FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
  LIMIT 1
)
UPDATE jobs SET status = 'running', started_at = now()
FROM job WHERE jobs.id = job.id
RETURNING jobs.*;</pre><p>Add a partial index <code>ON jobs (id) WHERE status = 'queued'</code>, keep the queue small (move or delete finished jobs), and tune autovacuum aggressively on it — dead tuples at the head of the queue are the classic reason queue polling slows down over time.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','queues'] },
{ q: `What are statement_timeout, lock_timeout and idle_in_transaction_session_timeout, and where should you set them?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>statement_timeout</code>: cancel any statement running longer than N. Set per role (<code>ALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = '30s'</code>), larger for reporting roles.</li><li><code>lock_timeout</code>: cancel a statement that waits for a lock longer than N. Essential for migrations: <code>SET lock_timeout = '3s'</code> before <code>ALTER TABLE</code> so a blocked DDL does not queue up all traffic behind it.</li><li><code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code>: terminate sessions idle inside an open transaction (they hold locks and the xmin horizon).</li><li><code>transaction_timeout</code> (PG 17+): cap total transaction duration.</li></ul><p>Avoid setting statement_timeout globally in <code>postgresql.conf</code> — it also hits maintenance jobs and pg_dump; prefer role or database level.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','gucs'] },
{ q: `How do you find out what the database is waiting on, beyond row locks?`,
  a: `<p>Sample <code>wait_event_type</code>/<code>wait_event</code> from <code>pg_stat_activity</code> every second or so and aggregate (this is what "active session history" tools do):</p><pre>SELECT wait_event_type, wait_event, count(*)
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE state = 'active' AND pid &lt;&gt; pg_backend_pid()
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC;</pre><ul><li>NULL wait event while active = on CPU.</li><li><code>IO: DataFileRead</code> — reading pages not in cache (missing index, working set larger than RAM).</li><li><code>LWLock: WALWrite</code> / <code>IO: WALSync</code> — commit-bound, slow WAL disk.</li><li><code>LWLock: BufferMapping</code> / <code>BufferContent</code> — contention on hot buffers.</li><li><code>LWLock: LockManager</code> — many relations (partitions, indexes) locked per query beyond the 16 fast-path slots (PG 18 scales fast-path slots with <code>max_locks_per_transaction</code>).</li><li><code>Client: ClientRead</code> — waiting for the application (idle in transaction).</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','wait-events'] },
{ q: `Plan-time vs run-time partition pruning — how do you tell which happened?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Plan-time pruning</strong> happens when the partition key is compared to constants (<code>order_date &gt;= '2025-01-01'</code>): pruned partitions do not appear in the plan at all. <strong>Run-time pruning</strong> happens when values are known only at execution: parameters of a generic plan, <code>now()</code>-based expressions (stable, not immutable) or values coming from a join (nested loop parameter). The plan lists all partitions, and EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows <code>Subplans Removed: N</code> or child nodes marked <code>(never executed)</code>.</p><p>Requirements: <code>enable_partition_pruning = on</code> (default), and the query must filter on the partition key itself — <code>WHERE date_trunc('month', order_date) = ...</code> or a cast on the key prevents pruning. Run-time pruning still pays planning cost for all partitions, so keep partition counts reasonable.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','planner'] },
{ q: `After partitioning a table, some queries got slower. Why?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Queries that do not filter on the partition key now scan every partition (an index scan per partition, merged) instead of one index.</li><li>Planning time and lock-manager overhead grow with partition count (each partition and each of its indexes is locked).</li><li>Unique constraints and primary keys must include the partition key, so lookups by <code>id</code> alone cannot use a single global index — PostgreSQL has no global indexes.</li><li>Too many small partitions (daily partitions for years) multiply all of the above.</li></ul><p>Partitioning is primarily a <strong>data management</strong> tool (drop old data instantly, vacuum and index smaller pieces), and a performance tool only when most queries prune to a few partitions. Pick the key from the dominant query filter and aim for partitions in the tens to low thousands, not tens of thousands.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning'] },
{ q: `What are partition-wise join and partition-wise aggregate?`,
  a: `<p>With <code>enable_partitionwise_join = on</code>, two tables partitioned identically on the join key are joined partition by partition (orders_2025_01 with order_items_2025_01, ...), so each join is small enough to hash in memory and can run in parallel. <code>enable_partitionwise_aggregate = on</code> pushes GROUP BY down to each partition (fully if grouping includes the partition key, otherwise partially with a final combine step). Both are <strong>off by default</strong> because they increase planning time and memory; enable them for analytical sessions that join or aggregate large partitioned tables.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','planner'] },
{ q: `Why is opening thousands of direct connections to PostgreSQL bad for performance?`,
  a: `<p>PostgreSQL uses a <strong>process per connection</strong>. Each backend costs several MB of private memory (more as its catalog and plan caches grow), process creation and authentication take milliseconds (much more with TLS and SCRAM), and with thousands of backends the system spends CPU on context switching, snapshot building (scales with the number of backends, much improved in PG 14) and lock contention. Throughput typically peaks at a few times the number of CPU cores of <em>active</em> connections and then declines.</p><p>Fix: keep <code>max_connections</code> in the hundreds and put a pooler in front — <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer or pgcat, an application-side pool (HikariCP, Npgsql pooling), or a cloud proxy (RDS Proxy).</p>`,
  tags: ['connections','pooling'] },
{ q: `Explain PgBouncer's pooling modes and their trade-offs.`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer has three modes:</p><ul><li><strong>Session</strong>: a server connection is assigned for the client's whole session. All features work, but little multiplexing.</li><li><strong>Transaction</strong>: a server connection is assigned per transaction and returned at COMMIT. Thousands of clients share tens of server connections. Session state does not survive between transactions: <code>SET</code> (use <code>SET LOCAL</code>), session advisory locks, <code>LISTEN</code>, temp tables kept across transactions, <code>WITH HOLD</code> cursors. Protocol-level prepared statements are supported since PgBouncer 1.21 via <code>max_prepared_statements</code>.</li><li><strong>Statement</strong>: returned after every statement; multi-statement transactions are forbidden.</li></ul><p>Transaction mode is the standard choice for web/OLTP workloads.</p>`,
  tags: ['connections','pgbouncer'] },
{ q: `Your app servers each hold 50 connections and throughput is falling. How do you right-size the connection pool?`,
  a: `<p>Start small: the database does useful work on roughly as many active queries as it has CPU cores (plus some for I/O waits). A widely used starting formula is <code>pool_size ≈ cores × 2 + effective_spindles</code>; on a 16-core server that is ~32–40 server connections, even for thousands of clients. Then measure: if CPU is low but clients wait on the pool, increase; if latency rises while CPU saturates, decrease.</p><p>Remember the total: with 20 app instances each holding a 20-connection pool you already have 400 connections. Put a shared pooler (PgBouncer) in front, set <code>max_connections</code> with headroom for admin and replication connections, and keep transactions short — a pool slot held during an HTTP call to another service is wasted capacity.</p>`,
  tags: ['connections','pooling'] },
{ q: `What does JIT compilation do in PostgreSQL and when does it hurt?`,
  a: `<p>Since PG 11 (on by default since PG 12) PostgreSQL can compile expressions and tuple deforming to machine code with LLVM when a query's estimated cost exceeds <code>jit_above_cost</code> (100000); higher thresholds enable inlining and optimization (<code>jit_inline_above_cost</code>, <code>jit_optimize_above_cost</code>, 500000). It helps long-running analytical queries that evaluate expressions over millions of rows.</p><p>It hurts when estimates are inflated: a query that actually runs 20 ms spends 100–500 ms compiling. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows a <code>JIT:</code> section with generation, inlining, optimization and emission times. Fixes: raise <code>jit_above_cost</code>, disable it for OLTP roles (<code>ALTER ROLE app SET jit = off</code>), and fix the misestimate.</p>`,
  tags: ['jit','planner'] },
{ q: `Which settings control parallel query and how do you tune them?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>max_worker_processes</code> (8, restart): total background workers.</li><li><code>max_parallel_workers</code> (8): cap on parallel workers across the server.</li><li><code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code> (2): per Gather node.</li><li><code>min_parallel_table_scan_size</code> (8MB), <code>min_parallel_index_scan_size</code> (512kB): below these no parallelism; workers increase logarithmically with size.</li><li><code>parallel_setup_cost</code> (1000), <code>parallel_tuple_cost</code> (0.1): planner costs.</li><li>Per table: <code>ALTER TABLE orders SET (parallel_workers = 6)</code>.</li></ul><p>On an analytical server, raise per-gather workers to 4–8; on busy OLTP servers keep it low, since parallel workers multiply CPU and work_mem usage per query and can starve concurrent queries.</p>`,
  tags: ['parallel','gucs'] },
{ q: `Why might a query not use parallel workers?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Table smaller than <code>min_parallel_table_scan_size</code> or estimated cost too low.</li><li>It calls a function marked <code>PARALLEL UNSAFE</code> (the default for user-defined functions!) — mark safe functions <code>PARALLEL SAFE</code>.</li><li>The statement writes data (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE; though <code>CREATE TABLE AS</code> and <code>SELECT INTO</code> can use parallel reads), runs inside a cursor, or is in a serializable transaction on older versions.</li><li>No free workers at execution time: <code>Workers Launched</code> lower than planned.</li><li><code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather = 0</code> for the role or session.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['parallel'] },
{ q: `What is the fastest way to bulk load 100 million rows?`,
  a: `<ol><li>Use <code>COPY</code> (or <code>\\copy</code> client-side), not INSERT — it avoids per-statement parsing and round trips.</li><li>Load into a table without secondary indexes, FKs and triggers; create indexes afterwards (sorted bulk build with high <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> and parallel maintenance workers) and add FKs with <code>NOT VALID</code> then <code>VALIDATE</code>.</li><li>Load in a transaction that also created or truncated the table with <code>wal_level = minimal</code> to skip WAL (not possible with replication), or use an <code>UNLOGGED</code> staging table and <code>INSERT ... SELECT</code> into the final table.</li><li>Use <code>COPY ... FREEZE</code> on a newly created/truncated table to avoid a later freezing vacuum.</li><li>Raise <code>max_wal_size</code> during the load, run several COPY streams in parallel into different partitions, and finish with <code>VACUUM (ANALYZE)</code>.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['bulk-load','copy'] },
{ q: `Why is inserting rows one at a time with autocommit slow?`,
  a: `<p>Each autocommit INSERT is a separate transaction: a network round trip, parse/plan, and a WAL flush (fsync) at commit. At 1 ms per commit you cap out near 1,000 rows/s per connection. Batching changes everything:</p><pre>INSERT INTO audit_logs (table_name, operation, row_id, changed_by)
VALUES ('orders', 'INSERT', 1, 'app'),
       ('orders', 'INSERT', 2, 'app'),
       ('orders', 'INSERT', 3, 'app');</pre><p>Multi-row VALUES (hundreds to a few thousand rows per statement), wrapping many inserts in one transaction, driver batching/pipelining, or <code>COPY</code> typically give 10–100× more throughput. Keep each batch's transaction reasonably short to avoid long lock and snapshot holding.</p>`,
  tags: ['writes','application'] },
{ q: `How do you delete 200 million old rows from a busy table?`,
  a: `<p>A single <code>DELETE</code> runs for hours, holds row locks, generates huge WAL (replica lag), leaves 200M dead tuples for VACUUM, and rolls back everything if it fails. Better options:</p><ul><li><strong>Partitioned table</strong>: <code>ALTER TABLE ... DETACH PARTITION ... CONCURRENTLY</code> then <code>DROP TABLE</code> — instant, no dead tuples. This is the strongest argument for time-based partitioning.</li><li><strong>Batches</strong> of 5–50k rows by primary key range, each in its own transaction, with a pause and monitoring of replica lag:</li></ul><pre>DELETE FROM audit_logs
WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM audit_logs
             WHERE changed_at &lt; now() - interval '1 year'
             ORDER BY id LIMIT 10000);</pre><ul><li>If keeping a small fraction: copy survivors to a new table, swap names in a short transaction.</li></ul><p>Run VACUUM afterwards (or let autovacuum) so space is reused.</p>`,
  tags: ['writes','bloat','partitioning'] },
{ q: `What are temporary files in PostgreSQL and how do you control them?`,
  a: `<p>When a sort, hash, or materialization exceeds its memory limit (<code>work_mem</code>), it spills to temp files under <code>base/pgsql_tmp</code> (or <code>temp_tablespaces</code>). Monitor with:</p><pre>SELECT datname, temp_files, pg_size_pretty(temp_bytes) AS temp_size
FROM pg_stat_database;</pre><p>Set <code>log_temp_files = 0</code> (or a size threshold) to log every spill with its query, and <code>temp_file_limit</code> to cap per-process temp usage so one runaway query cannot fill the disk. Fixes: add indexes that provide order, raise work_mem for the specific query, reduce the data before sorting, or avoid huge DISTINCT/ORDER BY over unnecessary columns.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','io'] },
{ q: `What changed for I/O in PostgreSQL 18?`,
  a: `<p>PG 18 introduced an <strong>asynchronous I/O subsystem</strong> controlled by <code>io_method</code>: <code>worker</code> (default, dedicated I/O worker processes, count set by <code>io_workers</code>), <code>io_uring</code> (Linux, if built with liburing) or <code>sync</code> (old behavior). Sequential scans, bitmap heap scans and VACUUM can issue reads ahead without waiting for each one, which helps most on cloud block storage with high latency. <code>effective_io_concurrency</code> (default raised to 16) and <code>maintenance_io_concurrency</code> control how many I/Os are in flight. Writes are still synchronous in PG 18.</p>`,
  tags: ['io','pg18'] },
{ q: `Why should you enable huge pages for a large shared_buffers?`,
  a: `<p>With 4KB pages, a 32GB shared memory segment needs ~8 million page table entries <em>per process</em> that maps it; with hundreds of backends, page tables alone consume gigabytes and TLB misses add CPU overhead. 2MB huge pages cut that by 512×. Set <code>huge_pages = on</code> (fail if unavailable) or <code>try</code> (default), reserve them in the OS (<code>vm.nr_hugepages</code>; PG 15+ can report the needed number via <code>postgres -C shared_memory_size_in_huge_pages</code>), and disable Transparent Huge Pages, which cause latency spikes.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','os'] },
{ q: `Random UUIDv4 vs UUIDv7 vs bigint as a primary key — what are the performance implications?`,
  a: `<p>Random UUIDv4 values insert at random positions in the B-tree: every insert touches a different leaf page, so the whole index must stay in cache, page splits leave pages half full, WAL grows (more full-page writes) and correlation with heap order is zero. A <code>bigint</code> identity or a <strong>time-ordered UUIDv7</strong> appends to the right edge of the index, keeping inserts cache-friendly.</p><pre>-- PG 18 core function
CREATE TABLE events (id uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuidv7(), payload jsonb);</pre><p>UUIDs are 16 bytes vs 8 for bigint, so indexes and FKs are larger. Choose bigint for internal keys, UUIDv7 when IDs must be generated client-side or be globally unique, and avoid v4 for large, write-heavy tables (before PG 18 generate v7 in the application or via an extension).</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','keys','pg18'] },
{ q: `What is the fastest way to get the latest row per group (e.g. last order per customer)?`,
  a: `<p>Three common patterns:</p><pre>-- 1. DISTINCT ON: one sort/scan, good when groups are small
SELECT DISTINCT ON (customer_id) customer_id, id, order_date
FROM orders ORDER BY customer_id, order_date DESC;

-- 2. LATERAL + LIMIT: best when there are few groups with many rows each
SELECT c.id, o.id, o.order_date
FROM customers c
CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
  SELECT id, order_date FROM orders
  WHERE customer_id = c.id ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 1) o;

-- 3. row_number() window: flexible (top-N), usually slowest</pre><p>With an index on <code>(customer_id, order_date DESC)</code>, the LATERAL form does one tiny index probe per customer. DISTINCT ON reads all orders once. Measure both; the right choice depends on rows-per-group.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','indexes'] },
{ q: `When do materialized views help performance, and what are their costs?`,
  a: `<p>A materialized view stores a query's result physically, so expensive aggregations (daily revenue per country over 50M orders) are read in milliseconds. It can be indexed. Costs: data is stale until <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code>, which recomputes the whole query (no built-in incremental refresh). Plain refresh takes an exclusive lock blocking reads; <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY</code> keeps it readable but requires a unique index and is slower (diff-based).</p><p>Alternatives: summary tables maintained incrementally by the application or triggers for near-real-time data, or the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_ivm for incremental view maintenance.</p>`,
  tags: ['materialized-views'] },
{ q: `How do function volatility and inlining affect query performance?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>VOLATILE</code> (default) functions are re-evaluated for every row and cannot be used in index conditions; <code>STABLE</code> functions can be evaluated once per scan for index lookups; <code>IMMUTABLE</code> can be pre-evaluated at plan time and used in expression indexes. Mislabeling for speed is dangerous (wrong results), but forgetting <code>STABLE</code> on a lookup function is a common cause of sequential scans.</li><li>Simple <code>LANGUAGE SQL</code> functions (single SELECT, not SECURITY DEFINER, no SET clauses) can be <strong>inlined</strong> into the calling query so the planner sees through them; PL/pgSQL functions are black boxes with a default estimated 1000 rows (set <code>ROWS</code> and <code>COST</code>).</li><li>Calling a PL/pgSQL function per row in a SELECT executes its queries per row — often a hidden N+1 inside the database.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['functions','planner'] },
{ q: `Why is SELECT * harmful on tables with large text or jsonb columns?`,
  a: `<p>Values larger than ~2KB are compressed and/or moved out of line into the table's TOAST relation. Selecting them forces detoasting: extra index lookups into the TOAST table, decompression, and transferring megabytes to the client. A list page that needs <code>id, title</code> but runs <code>SELECT *</code> on a <code>documents</code> table can be 10–100× slower. It also prevents index-only scans.</p><p>Select only needed columns, keep large blobs in a separate table if they are rarely read, and consider <code>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN body SET COMPRESSION lz4</code> (PG 14+) for faster compression/decompression than the default pglz.</p>`,
  tags: ['toast','sql'] },
{ q: `How does ORDER BY ... LIMIT use an index, and when can it backfire?`,
  a: `<p>With an index matching the ORDER BY, the plan is <code>Limit -&gt; Index Scan</code> and stops after N rows — top-N in milliseconds regardless of table size. Without one, it is a full scan plus top-N heapsort.</p><p>Backfire: <code>WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 10</code> with an index only on <code>order_date</code>. The planner may walk the date index backwards expecting to quickly find 10 rows for customer 42 (assuming even distribution). If that customer's orders are old or rare, it scans millions of entries. Fix with a composite index <code>(customer_id, order_date DESC)</code> that serves filter and sort together.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','planner'] },
{ q: `How do you benchmark PostgreSQL with pgbench?`,
  a: `<p><code>pgbench</code> is a client program shipped with core PostgreSQL. Built-in mode initializes TPC-B-like tables (<code>pgbench -i -s 100</code>) and runs a mixed workload. More useful in practice are custom scripts that reproduce your hot queries:</p><pre>-- custom.sql
\\set cid random(1, 500)
SELECT id, total_amount FROM orders WHERE customer_id = :cid ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 10;

pgbench -n -f custom.sql -c 32 -j 8 -T 120 -P 10 company_db</pre><p>Report TPS and latency percentiles (<code>--log</code>, <code>-r</code> for per-statement latency), test at several client counts to find the throughput knee, run long enough to include checkpoints, and change one variable at a time.</p>`,
  tags: ['benchmarking'] },
{ q: `When is a hash index useful?`,
  a: `<p>Hash indexes support only equality (<code>=</code>). Since PG 10 they are WAL-logged and crash-safe. They store a 4-byte hash instead of the key, so for long keys (URLs, long text tokens) they can be much smaller than a B-tree, and lookups are O(1). They cannot enforce uniqueness, support ranges, sorting, multi-column keys or index-only scans. In most cases a B-tree is the better default; consider hash for equality-only lookups on wide values.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX sessions_token_hash ON sessions USING hash (token);</pre>`,
  tags: ['indexes'] },
{ q: `How can you find tables that are probably missing an index?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT relname, seq_scan, seq_tup_read,
       seq_tup_read / nullif(seq_scan, 0) AS avg_rows_per_seq_scan,
       idx_scan, n_live_tup
FROM pg_stat_user_tables
WHERE seq_scan &gt; 0
ORDER BY seq_tup_read DESC
LIMIT 15;</pre><p>Large tables with many sequential scans reading millions of rows each are candidates. Then find the actual queries in <code>pg_stat_statements</code> (high <code>shared_blks_read</code>/<code>shared_blks_hit</code> per call) and EXPLAIN them. Do not add indexes from this view alone: some sequential scans are correct (reports reading most of the table, small tables).</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','monitoring'] },
{ q: `Is an index on a boolean or low-cardinality status column useful?`,
  a: `<p>A full B-tree on <code>is_active</code> (95% true) is nearly useless: for <code>true</code> the planner will scan the table anyway, and you pay write cost for every row. But if you query the rare value, a <strong>partial index</strong> is excellent:</p><pre>CREATE INDEX orders_pending ON orders (order_date) WHERE status = 'pending';</pre><p>Alternatively put the low-cardinality column first in a composite index that also contains a selective column, or rely on PG 18 skip scan when the leading column has few distinct values and is not constrained.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','partial-index'] },
{ q: `Why is ORDER BY random() LIMIT 1 slow, and how do you sample efficiently?`,
  a: `<p><code>ORDER BY random()</code> computes a random value for every row and sorts the whole table to return one row. Alternatives:</p><pre>-- core TABLESAMPLE: ~1% of pages (fast, clustered sample)
SELECT * FROM orders TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM (1) LIMIT 100;
-- ~1% of rows, more uniform, reads all pages
SELECT * FROM orders TABLESAMPLE BERNOULLI (1);
-- random id within the id range (gaps bias the result slightly)
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id &gt;= (SELECT floor(random() * max(id))::bigint FROM orders)
ORDER BY id LIMIT 1;</pre><p>The <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>tsm_system_rows</code> adds <code>TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM_ROWS(n)</code> for an exact row count.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','sampling'] },
{ q: `You need to UPDATE a column on every row of a 300M-row table. What happens and how do you do it efficiently?`,
  a: `<p>MVCC turns each UPDATE into a new tuple version: a single statement roughly doubles the table size (old versions remain until VACUUM), writes every index entry again unless the update is HOT, generates WAL comparable to the table size (replicas lag), and holds row locks on all rows until commit.</p><p>Better approaches:</p><ul><li>Batch by primary key ranges (e.g. 50k rows per transaction), VACUUM between batches so freed space is reused and the table does not double.</li><li>Skip rows that already have the target value (<code>WHERE col IS DISTINCT FROM new_value</code>).</li><li>For a new column with a constant default, use <code>ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT x</code> — metadata-only since PG 11, no rewrite.</li><li>If transforming most columns, build a new table with <code>CREATE TABLE AS</code>/<code>INSERT ... SELECT</code> and swap, which avoids dead tuples entirely (needs a write freeze or change capture).</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['writes','mvcc','bloat'] },
]);

PGM.INTERVIEW.scenario = [
{ q: `Production CPU is at 100% on the primary. What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>Goal: find out <em>what</em> is burning CPU before changing anything, stabilize, then fix the root cause.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm it is PostgreSQL</strong> (<code>top</code>/<code>pidstat</code>): many postgres backends at high CPU vs one process, or something else (backup compression, antivirus).</li><li><strong>See what is running now</strong>:<pre>SELECT pid, now() - query_start AS runtime, state, wait_event_type, wait_event,
       left(query, 100) AS query
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE state = 'active' AND pid &lt;&gt; pg_backend_pid()
ORDER BY runtime DESC;</pre>Map a hot OS PID from <code>top</code> to a row here.</li><li><strong>Find the heaviest query shapes</strong> with the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_stat_statements:<pre>SELECT queryid, calls, round(total_exec_time) AS total_ms,
       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2) AS mean_ms, left(query, 80)
FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 10;</pre>Take two snapshots a minute apart and diff them to see what is hot <em>right now</em>.</li><li><strong>Stabilize</strong>: cancel runaway queries (<code>SELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid)</code>), pause a misbehaving batch job, or throttle the pool. Do not restart the server blindly — the load usually comes back.</li><li><strong>Diagnose the culprit</strong>: <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on it. Typical causes: a plan flip to a sequential scan or nested loop after stale stats, a new deploy with a missing index or an N+1 loop, a sudden traffic spike, JIT compiling on short queries, or a connection storm.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: add the index (<code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>), <code>ANALYZE</code> the table, roll back the deploy, or disable JIT for the role.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: alert on CPU and on per-query time from pg_stat_statements; enable <code>auto_explain</code> for slow statements; set <code>statement_timeout</code> per role.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['cpu','incident'] },
{ q: `A streaming replica is 2 hours behind the primary. How do you diagnose and fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure where the lag is</strong> on the primary:<pre>SELECT application_name, state, sent_lsn, write_lsn, flush_lsn, replay_lsn,
       write_lag, flush_lag, replay_lag,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), replay_lsn)) AS replay_gap
FROM pg_stat_replication;</pre>On the replica: <code>SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp();</code> (note it also grows when the primary is idle).</li><li><strong>Classify</strong>: if <code>sent_lsn</code> is far behind the current LSN, it is the <strong>network or WAL sender</strong>; if received but not replayed (<code>replay_lsn</code> behind <code>flush_lsn</code>), it is <strong>replay</strong> on the standby.</li><li><strong>Replay blocked by queries</strong>: long queries on the replica with <code>max_standby_streaming_delay = -1</code> or high, or <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> combined with conflicts. Check <code>pg_stat_activity</code> on the replica and <code>pg_stat_database_conflicts</code>.</li><li><strong>Replay too slow</strong>: replay is single-threaded; a huge batch UPDATE/DELETE, index rebuild or <code>VACUUM FULL</code> on the primary generates WAL faster than one process can apply. Check replica I/O (slower disk than the primary is common) and CPU of the startup process.</li><li><strong>Network</strong>: bandwidth to a remote DR site; enable <code>wal_compression</code> on the primary.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: cancel the blocking replica queries, give the replica equivalent storage, split bulk jobs into batches with lag checks, and if it is hopelessly behind and the WAL is gone, rebuild it with <code>pg_basebackup</code> (or a <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> such as pgBackRest restore).</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: alert on <code>replay_lag</code>, route read-after-write traffic to the primary, and use a dedicated reporting replica with relaxed delay settings.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['replication','incident'] },
{ q: `The disk is filling up because pg_wal keeps growing. What do you check and how do you fix it safely?`,
  a: `<p>WAL is retained for exactly three reasons: an <strong>archive_command that is failing</strong>, a <strong>replication slot</strong> that is not advancing, or <strong>settings</strong> (<code>max_wal_size</code>, <code>wal_keep_size</code>). Never delete files from pg_wal by hand — that corrupts the cluster.</p><ol><li><strong>Archiving</strong>:<pre>SELECT archived_count, failed_count, last_archived_wal, last_failed_wal, last_failed_time
FROM pg_stat_archiver;</pre>A rising <code>failed_count</code> means WAL piles up in pg_wal (see <code>.ready</code> files in <code>pg_wal/archive_status</code>). Read the server log for the archive command's error (full backup bucket, expired credentials) and fix it; the backlog drains automatically.</li><li><strong>Replication slots</strong>:<pre>SELECT slot_name, slot_type, active, wal_status,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained
FROM pg_replication_slots ORDER BY 5 DESC;</pre>An inactive slot from a decommissioned replica or a stopped CDC consumer retains WAL forever. If it is truly abandoned: <code>SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('old_slot');</code>.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong>: check <code>wal_keep_size</code> and <code>max_wal_size</code> for unreasonable values.</li><li><strong>Emergency space</strong>: extend the volume, or free space elsewhere (old logs). Then <code>CHECKPOINT;</code> lets PostgreSQL recycle segments no longer needed.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: set <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> (PG 13+) so a dead slot is invalidated instead of filling the disk, alert on pg_wal size, slot retained bytes and archiver failures, and put pg_wal on a volume with headroom.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['wal','disk','incident'] },
{ q: `Right after a deploy, the logs show many "deadlock detected" errors. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Read the deadlock report</strong> in the server log: PostgreSQL prints both processes, the lock each waited for and the statements, e.g. "Process 123 waits for ShareLock on transaction 456; blocked by process 789". Set <code>log_lock_waits = on</code> for more context.</li><li><strong>Correlate with the deploy diff</strong>: new code typically touches the same rows in a different order (one path updates <code>orders</code> then <code>payments</code>, another the reverse), adds a batch update iterating rows in arbitrary order, or adds a trigger/FK that locks a parent row.</li><li><strong>Immediate relief</strong>: roll back the deploy or disable the new job; make sure the application retries deadlock failures (SQLSTATE <code>40P01</code>) — PostgreSQL already aborted one victim transaction.</li><li><strong>Root-cause fix</strong>: lock rows in a consistent order:<pre>SELECT id FROM orders WHERE id = ANY($1) ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE orders SET status = 'paid' WHERE id = ANY($1);</pre>Keep transactions short, avoid user think-time inside transactions, and for FK-related deadlocks note that inserting child rows takes <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code> on the parent.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong>: monitor <code>SELECT deadlocks FROM pg_stat_database WHERE datname = current_database();</code> after the fix.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['locks','deadlocks','incident'] },
{ q: `Queries on a 500M-row orders table have become slow. How do you approach it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Identify which queries</strong> are slow (pg_stat_statements <span class="tag ext">extension</span>) and whether they degraded gradually (growth, bloat) or suddenly (plan change, deploy).</li><li><strong>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</strong> each: look for sequential scans, estimate vs actual row mismatches, sorts/hash spilling to disk, and high Heap Fetches on index-only scans.</li><li><strong>Check table health</strong>:<pre>SELECT n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum, last_autoanalyze,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('orders')) AS total_size
FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname = 'orders';</pre>Tune per-table autovacuum (scale factor 0.01–0.02) if dead tuples are high.</li><li><strong>Index design</strong> for the dominant access paths: composite <code>(customer_id, order_date DESC)</code>, covering indexes with <code>INCLUDE</code>, partial indexes for hot subsets (<code>WHERE status = 'pending'</code>), BRIN on <code>order_date</code> for range reports. Build with <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>.</li><li><strong>Query shape</strong>: replace OFFSET pagination with keyset, avoid SELECT *, fix non-sargable predicates.</li><li><strong>Partition by order_date</strong> (monthly) if most queries filter by time and old data must be archived: pruning keeps scans small, and retention becomes <code>DETACH/DROP PARTITION</code>. Migrate with a new partitioned table and batched copy, or attach the old table as a partition.</li><li><strong>Offload</strong> analytics to a replica, a summary table, or a columnar/OLAP system; only then consider sharding (e.g. the Citus <span class="tag ext">extension</span>).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['performance','partitioning','indexes'] },
{ q: `You see "WARNING: database "app" must be vacuumed within 10,000,000 transactions". What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>This is a transaction ID wraparound warning. At 3 million remaining XIDs PostgreSQL refuses new XID-consuming commands to protect data. Act immediately.</p><ol><li><strong>Find the oldest objects</strong>:<pre>SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid) FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC;
SELECT c.oid::regclass AS table_name, age(c.relfrozenxid) AS xid_age,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(c.oid)) AS size
FROM pg_class c WHERE c.relkind IN ('r','m','t')
ORDER BY age(c.relfrozenxid) DESC LIMIT 20;</pre></li><li><strong>Remove what blocks freezing</strong>: VACUUM cannot freeze past the oldest xmin horizon. Check long transactions, abandoned prepared transactions and stale replication slots:<pre>SELECT pid, xact_start, state, backend_xmin FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE backend_xmin IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age(backend_xmin) DESC LIMIT 5;
SELECT gid, prepared FROM pg_prepared_xacts;
SELECT slot_name, xmin, catalog_xmin FROM pg_replication_slots;</pre>Terminate/rollback/drop them as appropriate; also check <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> replicas with long queries.</li><li><strong>Vacuum the oldest tables first</strong>, manually: <code>VACUUM (FREEZE, VERBOSE) big_table;</code> — and for speed <code>SET vacuum_cost_delay = 0</code> and a large <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> in that session; <code>VACUUM (INDEX_CLEANUP OFF)</code> skips index work in an emergency. A running anti-wraparound autovacuum should not be killed — it restarts.</li><li><strong>If already in the stop state</strong>: run <code>VACUUM</code> in the affected database (PG 14+ failsafe kicks in automatically; single-user mode is no longer the recommended first step).</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: alert when <code>age(datfrozenxid)</code> exceeds ~500M, tune autovacuum throughput, and keep transactions short.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','wraparound','incident'] },
{ q: `A migration ran ALTER TABLE on a hot table and now the whole application is hanging. What happened and what do you do?`,
  a: `<p>Most ALTER TABLE forms need an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock. If one long-running transaction holds even a weak lock on the table, the ALTER waits — and every new query on that table queues <strong>behind the ALTER</strong>, so the app stalls even though the ALTER itself would be instant.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm the lock queue</strong>:<pre>SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, state,
       now() - xact_start AS xact_age, left(query, 80)
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE datname = current_database() AND wait_event_type = 'Lock';</pre></li><li><strong>Unblock immediately</strong>: cancel the ALTER (<code>SELECT pg_cancel_backend(&lt;alter_pid&gt;)</code>) so traffic resumes; or, if the migration is short and the blocker is an idle-in-transaction session, terminate the blocker.</li><li><strong>If the ALTER is itself rewriting the table</strong> (type change, volatile default on old versions), it holds the exclusive lock for the full rewrite — cancel it and redesign the migration.</li><li><strong>Re-run safely</strong>:<pre>SET lock_timeout = '3s';
SET statement_timeout = '15s';
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN source text;</pre>with retries in the migration tool, during low traffic.</li><li><strong>Use non-blocking variants</strong>: <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>, <code>ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID</code> + <code>VALIDATE CONSTRAINT</code>, expand/contract for type changes.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['locks','migrations','incident'] },
{ q: `The application gets "FATAL: sorry, too many clients already". What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Get in</strong>: superusers have <code>superuser_reserved_connections</code> (3) and PG 16+ roles with <code>pg_use_reserved_connections</code> have <code>reserved_connections</code>.</li><li><strong>See who holds connections</strong>:<pre>SELECT usename, application_name, client_addr, state, count(*)
FROM pg_stat_activity
GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4 ORDER BY 5 DESC;</pre>Typical findings: hundreds of <code>idle</code> connections from oversized app pools, <code>idle in transaction</code> sessions leaked by code paths without commit/rollback, or a new service deployed with its own pool.</li><li><strong>Relieve</strong>: terminate idle leaks:<pre>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE state = 'idle in transaction' AND now() - state_change &gt; interval '10 minutes';</pre></li><li><strong>Do not just raise max_connections</strong> to 5000: each backend costs memory and CPU, and it needs a restart.</li><li><strong>Fix structurally</strong>: put <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer in transaction mode in front, shrink per-instance pool sizes so (instances × pool) stays below the limit, set <code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code> and <code>idle_session_timeout</code>, and set per-role limits (<code>ALTER ROLE batch CONNECTION LIMIT 20</code>).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['connections','incident'] },
{ q: `A query is fast in staging but slow in production. How do you find out why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Compare plans</strong>, not timings: run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> in both with the <em>same parameter values</em> the app uses.</li><li><strong>Data volume and distribution</strong>: staging often has 1% of the data and uniform values; production has skew (one customer with 2M orders). Check <code>pg_class.reltuples</code> and <code>pg_stats</code> for the filter columns in both.</li><li><strong>Statistics freshness</strong>: <code>last_analyze</code>/<code>last_autoanalyze</code> in <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code>; run <code>ANALYZE</code>.</li><li><strong>Configuration</strong> differences:<pre>SELECT name, setting FROM pg_settings
WHERE source &lt;&gt; 'default'
ORDER BY name;</pre>Compare work_mem, random_page_cost, effective_cache_size, jit, plan_cache_mode, and role-level settings (<code>pg_db_role_setting</code>).</li><li><strong>Generic plans</strong>: the app uses prepared statements; after 5 executions a generic plan may be chosen. Reproduce with <code>PREPARE</code> or <code>EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN)</code>; fix with <code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code> for the role if needed.</li><li><strong>Bloat and cache</strong>: production tables may be bloated or not cached; compare <code>shared read</code> vs <code>hit</code> buffers.</li><li><strong>Concurrency</strong>: locks and CPU saturation exist only under real load — check <code>wait_event</code> during the slow runs.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['planner','troubleshooting'] },
{ q: `Autovacuum cannot keep up: dead tuples keep climbing on several large tables. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>:<pre>SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum, autovacuum_count
FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 10;
SELECT pid, relid::regclass, phase, heap_blks_scanned, heap_blks_total, index_vacuum_count
FROM pg_stat_progress_vacuum;</pre></li><li><strong>Check the xmin horizon first</strong>: if a long transaction, idle-in-transaction session, old replication slot or replica with <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> holds back xmin, VACUUM runs but cannot remove anything (VACUUM VERBOSE reports "dead but not yet removable"). Fix that before tuning.</li><li><strong>Throughput</strong>: autovacuum is throttled by default (cost limit 200 shared across workers). Raise <code>autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit</code> (e.g. 2000) and keep <code>autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay</code> at 2ms; on fast SSDs this is the biggest win.</li><li><strong>Workers</strong>: if all <code>autovacuum_max_workers</code> are busy on huge tables, small hot tables starve; raise workers (restart needed before PG 18) together with the cost limit.</li><li><strong>Trigger earlier per table</strong>: <code>ALTER TABLE orders SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.01, autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit = 3000);</code></li><li><strong>Memory</strong>: raise <code>autovacuum_work_mem</code> so each pass scans indexes fewer times (less relevant on PG 17+).</li><li><strong>Reduce the source</strong>: fewer needless updates, HOT-friendly design (fillfactor, fewer indexes), partitioning so old partitions stay frozen.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['autovacuum','vacuum'] },
{ q: `Someone ran DELETE FROM customers without a WHERE clause in production and committed. How do you recover?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Stop the damage</strong>: block further writes if the delete cascaded (ON DELETE CASCADE) or the app is writing on top of inconsistent data; note the exact time of the DELETE (from logs, <code>pg_stat_activity</code> history, or audit logs).</li><li><strong>Do not VACUUM the table</strong>: the deleted tuples are still physically present until vacuum removes them. Consider pausing autovacuum on it: <code>ALTER TABLE customers SET (autovacuum_enabled = false);</code> — they are only recoverable with special tools, though, so do not rely on this.</li><li><strong>Point-in-time recovery</strong> (the reliable way): restore the latest base backup to a <em>separate</em> server and replay WAL up to just before the delete:<pre>restore_command = 'pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-get %f "%p"'
recovery_target_time = '2025-10-01 14:31:59+05:30'
recovery_target_action = 'pause'</pre>(here using the <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest; plain <code>cp</code> from an archive works too).</li><li><strong>Copy the lost rows back</strong> from the recovered instance into production, e.g. export with <code>COPY customers TO STDOUT</code> or via <code>postgres_fdw</code>, then <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code>, including cascaded child tables in FK order.</li><li><strong>Alternatives</strong>: a delayed replica (<code>recovery_min_apply_delay = '1h'</code>) lets you pause and extract quickly; audit/trigger history tables may hold <code>old_data</code>; a logical dump if recent enough.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: no direct DML access in production, <code>BEGIN</code> first in psql, set the psql variable <code>ON_ERROR_ROLLBACK</code>/autocommit off for humans, and regularly test PITR.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['backup','pitr','incident'] },
{ q: `An unused replication slot left by a decommissioned CDC tool is retaining 400GB of WAL. How do you handle it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong> it is inactive and abandoned:<pre>SELECT slot_name, plugin, slot_type, active, active_pid, wal_status,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained
FROM pg_replication_slots;</pre></li><li><strong>Check with the owner team</strong>: dropping a logical slot loses the consumer's position; they would need a fresh snapshot to resume. For physical slots, the replica would need rebuilding.</li><li><strong>Drop it</strong>: <code>SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('debezium_old');</code> (it must not be active; terminate <code>active_pid</code> first if a zombie consumer holds it). WAL segments are recycled at the next checkpoint.</li><li><strong>Check the xmin effects</strong>: a logical slot's <code>catalog_xmin</code> also holds back vacuum of system catalogs — catalog bloat improves after dropping it.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: set <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> (e.g. 100GB) so a lagging slot gets invalidated (<code>wal_status = 'lost'</code>) instead of filling the disk, set <code>idle_replication_slot_timeout</code> (PG 18), and alert on slot retained bytes.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['replication','wal'] },
{ q: `A critical query's plan flipped overnight and it went from 50 ms to 40 s. Nothing was deployed. What happened?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Capture the bad plan</strong> with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> (or from <code>auto_explain</code> logs) and compare with the known-good plan (from staging or logs).</li><li><strong>Look for an estimate flip</strong>: a node where estimated rows differ from actual by 100×+. The usual trigger is <strong>auto-ANALYZE</strong> taking a new random sample — statistics crossed a threshold (a value dropped out of the MCV list, histogram changed) and the planner switched join type or index.</li><li><strong>Check data changes</strong>: a big load or purge, a new tenant with skewed data, or the partition for the new month just created and empty.</li><li><strong>Immediate fix</strong>: <code>ANALYZE orders;</code> sometimes flips it back; if not, adjust what the planner needs:<pre>ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN customer_id SET STATISTICS 1000;
CREATE STATISTICS orders_cust_status (dependencies, mcv) ON customer_id, status FROM orders;
ANALYZE orders;</pre></li><li><strong>Make the good plan structurally obvious</strong>: a composite index matching filter + sort, rewriting the query, or for prepared statements <code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code>.</li><li><strong>Last resort</strong>: plan hints via the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_hint_plan — PostgreSQL core has no hints.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: auto_explain with a duration threshold so regressions are captured, and alert on p95 latency per queryid.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['planner','statistics','incident'] },
{ q: `Dozens of sessions show "idle in transaction" for hours. Why is that dangerous and what do you do?`,
  a: `<p>An idle-in-transaction session keeps its locks (blocking DDL and conflicting writes) and its snapshot, which holds back the <strong>xmin horizon</strong>: VACUUM cannot remove any dead tuple newer than it, across the whole database. Hours of this means bloat everywhere and degraded index performance.</p><ol><li><strong>Find them</strong>:<pre>SELECT pid, usename, application_name, now() - xact_start AS xact_age,
       now() - state_change AS idle_for, left(query, 80) AS last_query
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE state = 'idle in transaction'
ORDER BY xact_start;</pre>The last query hints at the code path (a SELECT followed by a forgotten commit, an exception handler without rollback, an HTTP call inside a transaction).</li><li><strong>Terminate</strong> the old ones: <code>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid)</code>.</li><li><strong>Guardrail</strong>: <code>ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '60s';</code></li><li><strong>Fix the application</strong>: transaction scopes in try/finally, no external calls inside transactions, ORM settings that don't open implicit transactions for reads.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['transactions','vacuum','locks'] },
{ q: `You need to add an index on a 1 TB table in production without downtime. How?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Check resources</strong>: free disk for the index size (plus temp sort files), I/O headroom, and replication lag tolerance (index build WAL streams to replicas).</li><li><strong>Build concurrently</strong> with generous memory in that session:<pre>SET maintenance_work_mem = '4GB';
SET max_parallel_maintenance_workers = 4;
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_date
  ON orders (customer_id, order_date DESC);</pre>It does not block reads or writes but takes ~2–3× longer, waits for existing transactions at its phases, and cannot run inside a transaction block.</li><li><strong>Monitor</strong>: <code>SELECT phase, blocks_done, blocks_total FROM pg_stat_progress_create_index;</code></li><li><strong>Verify validity</strong>: if it fails (deadlock, unique violation, cancel), it leaves an <code>INVALID</code> index that still slows writes:<pre>SELECT indexrelid::regclass FROM pg_index WHERE NOT indisvalid;</pre>Drop it with <code>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> and retry.</li><li><strong>For partitioned tables</strong>: <code>CREATE INDEX ON ONLY parent</code> (invalid), then <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> on each partition and <code>ALTER INDEX parent_idx ATTACH PARTITION part_idx</code>.</li><li><strong>Afterwards</strong>: confirm usage with EXPLAIN and <code>pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan</code>.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['indexes','migrations'] },
{ q: `You must add a NOT NULL column with a default to a 300M-row table without blocking the application.`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Constant default</strong>: since PG 11, <code>ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT</code> with a non-volatile default is metadata-only (the default is stored in the catalog and returned for old rows):<pre>SET lock_timeout = '3s';
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN channel text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'web';</pre>It still needs a brief ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, hence <code>lock_timeout</code> and retries.</li><li><strong>Volatile default</strong> (<code>DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</code>, <code>clock_timestamp()</code>) forces a full table rewrite under the exclusive lock — avoid. Instead: add the column nullable without default, set a default for new rows, backfill in batches, then enforce NOT NULL.</li><li><strong>Enforcing NOT NULL online</strong> on an existing column:<pre>ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_channel_nn CHECK (channel IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_channel_nn;   -- no write blocking
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN channel SET NOT NULL;      -- PG 12+ uses the check, no scan
ALTER TABLE orders DROP CONSTRAINT orders_channel_nn;</pre></li><li><strong>Batched backfill</strong>: update by PK ranges of ~10–50k rows, sleeping between batches and watching replica lag and dead tuples.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['migrations','ddl'] },
{ q: `An int4 primary key is at 2.0 billion and will overflow in weeks. How do you migrate to bigint without downtime?`,
  a: `<p><code>ALTER COLUMN id TYPE bigint</code> rewrites the table and all indexes under an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock — hours of downtime on a big table. Use expand/contract:</p><ol><li><strong>Check headroom</strong>:<pre>SELECT last_value, 2147483647 - last_value AS remaining FROM orders_id_seq;</pre></li><li><strong>Add a new column</strong> <code>id_new bigint</code> and a trigger that copies <code>NEW.id</code> into it on INSERT/UPDATE.</li><li><strong>Backfill</strong> existing rows in batches by id range.</li><li><strong>Build the unique index concurrently</strong>: <code>CREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_id_new_uq ON orders (id_new);</code> and add <code>NOT NULL</code> via a validated CHECK constraint.</li><li><strong>Referencing FKs</strong>: child columns (<code>order_items.order_id</code>) must also become bigint — same procedure for each.</li><li><strong>Swap in one short transaction</strong> (with lock_timeout): drop the old PK constraint, <code>ADD CONSTRAINT orders_pkey PRIMARY KEY USING INDEX orders_id_new_uq</code>, rename columns, alter the sequence to <code>AS bigint</code>, move the default/identity, recreate FKs as <code>NOT VALID</code> then validate.</li><li><strong>Short-term escape hatch</strong> if time runs out: restart the sequence at negative values (<code>-2147483648</code>, increasing) to buy time — only if the app tolerates negative ids.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: use bigint for all surrogate keys; monitor sequence usage.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['migrations','keys'] },
{ q: `The primary database server has died. How does failover work and what do you check?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong> the primary is really down (not a network partition between monitor and DB) — a false failover creates split brain.</li><li><strong>With an HA manager</strong> (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> Patroni with etcd/Consul, or pg_auto_failover, or the cloud provider's managed failover), the leader lock expires and the most up-to-date healthy replica is promoted automatically; check <code>patronictl list</code> for the new leader and timeline.</li><li><strong>Manual failover</strong>: pick the replica with the highest received LSN (<code>SELECT pg_last_wal_receive_lsn();</code> on each), then promote it: <code>SELECT pg_promote();</code></li><li><strong>Fence the old primary</strong> so it cannot come back as a writer (STONITH, stop the service, remove from load balancer).</li><li><strong>Redirect clients</strong>: DNS/VIP, HAProxy health checks, PgBouncer config, or multi-host connection strings with <code>target_session_attrs=read-write</code>.</li><li><strong>Re-point other replicas</strong> to the new primary (Patroni does this), and rebuild the old primary as a replica with <code>pg_rewind</code> (needs <code>wal_log_hints</code> or data checksums) or a fresh base backup.</li><li><strong>Assess data loss</strong>: with asynchronous replication, transactions committed on the old primary but not yet shipped are lost; with <code>synchronous_standby_names</code> and <code>synchronous_commit = on</code> they are not.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['ha','failover','incident'] },
{ q: `After a network partition, two nodes both believe they are the primary. What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>Split brain: both nodes accepted writes, so their histories diverged.</p><ol><li><strong>Stop writes on one immediately</strong>: decide which node is authoritative (usually the one the HA system promoted and clients mostly used) and shut down or isolate the other.</li><li><strong>Quantify divergence</strong>: compare timelines and LSNs; find rows written to the losing node after the fork (via <code>updated_at</code> columns, audit tables, or logical decoding of its WAL since the fork LSN).</li><li><strong>Reconcile manually</strong>: export changed rows from the loser and merge into the winner with business rules — there is no automatic merge.</li><li><strong>Rebuild the losing node</strong> as a replica: <code>pg_rewind</code> to the winner's timeline or a new base backup.</li><li><strong>Fix the root cause</strong>: use a consensus-based HA manager (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> Patroni with a DCS of at least 3 members), fencing/watchdog so a node that loses the leader lock demotes itself, and never let two systems (a script and a manager) perform failover.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['ha','split-brain'] },
{ q: `A bad UPDATE ran at 14:05 and corrupted prices for thousands of products. Walk through a point-in-time recovery.`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Determine the exact moment</strong> just before the bad transaction: application logs, <code>log_statement = 'mod'</code> logs, or the transaction ID from audit logs (<code>recovery_target_xid</code> is most precise).</li><li><strong>Choose the base backup</strong> taken before 14:05 and make sure the WAL archive has every segment from that backup to the target.</li><li><strong>Restore to a separate host</strong> (never over production):<pre># postgresql.conf on the recovery host
restore_command = 'cp /archive/%f "%p"'
recovery_target_time = '2025-10-01 14:04:59+05:30'
recovery_target_inclusive = false
recovery_target_action = 'pause'
# then create recovery.signal and start</pre></li><li><strong>Verify</strong> in the paused instance that prices are correct (<code>SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();</code> and query the data). If too early/late, restart with a different target.</li><li><strong>Repair production</strong> surgically: export the correct rows (<code>COPY (SELECT id, price FROM products) TO ...</code>) and update production by id — this keeps all legitimate changes made after 14:05.</li><li><strong>Postmortem</strong>: why did an unbounded UPDATE reach production, and how long did recovery take versus your RTO?</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['pitr','backup'] },
{ q: `You discover backups have been "succeeding" for a year but have never been restored. What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>An untested backup is a hope, not a backup. Treat this as a risk to fix this week.</p><ol><li><strong>Inventory</strong>: what is backed up (logical <code>pg_dump</code> vs physical base backups + WAL archive), where, retention, encryption, and whether WAL archiving is continuous (<code>pg_stat_archiver</code>).</li><li><strong>Do a full restore now</strong> to an isolated host: restore the latest base backup, replay WAL to a recent point, start PostgreSQL, and check it with real queries (row counts of key tables, latest <code>order_date</code>).</li><li><strong>Verify integrity</strong>: <code>pg_verifybackup</code> for backups taken with <code>pg_basebackup</code> (PG 13+), <code>pg_amcheck</code> for B-tree and heap consistency on the restored copy, and for <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest, <code>pgbackrest verify</code>.</li><li><strong>Measure RTO and RPO</strong>: how long did the restore take, and how much data would have been lost? Compare with business requirements.</li><li><strong>Automate</strong>: a scheduled job that restores the latest backup weekly, runs checks, and alerts on failure; test PITR to a random timestamp periodically.</li><li><strong>Document the runbook</strong> so anyone on call can restore under pressure.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['backup','operations'] },
{ q: `You must upgrade a 3 TB database from PostgreSQL 13 to 17 with minimal downtime. What options do you have?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>pg_upgrade --link</strong> (core): reuses data files via hard links, so a multi-TB cluster upgrades in minutes. Downtime = stop old, run pg_upgrade, start new, then <code>vacuumdb --all --analyze-in-stages</code> (statistics are not carried before PG 18). Replicas must be rebuilt or upgraded with rsync. Rollback is hard once the new cluster has started with <code>--link</code>, so take a backup first.</li><li><strong>Logical replication</strong> (near-zero downtime): build a PG 17 cluster, copy the schema (<code>pg_dump --schema-only</code>), <code>CREATE PUBLICATION</code> on 13 and <code>CREATE SUBSCRIPTION</code> on 17, wait for the initial copy and catch-up, then cut over by stopping writes, waiting for lag 0, syncing sequences (not replicated), and switching connections. Caveats: tables need primary keys or replica identity, DDL and large objects are not replicated.</li><li><strong>Dump/restore</strong>: simplest but downtime proportional to size (hours for 3 TB even with <code>-j</code>).</li><li><strong>Preparation either way</strong>: test the upgrade on a restored copy, check extension versions available for 17, run <code>pg_upgrade --check</code>, review release notes for incompatibilities, and benchmark key queries on the new version.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['upgrade','replication'] },
{ q: `A heavily updated table is 3x larger than its live data. How do you shrink it without downtime?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure</strong> real bloat: <code>SELECT * FROM pgstattuple_approx('orders');</code> (contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgstattuple) — look at dead tuple and free space percentages.</li><li><strong>Find why</strong> it bloated: long transactions holding xmin, autovacuum too slow for the update rate, a one-off mass update. Fix the cause first or it will bloat again.</li><li><strong>Avoid VACUUM FULL</strong> on a live table: it holds ACCESS EXCLUSIVE for the whole rewrite.</li><li><strong>Rewrite online</strong> with the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> + client tool <code>pg_repack</code>:<pre>CREATE EXTENSION pg_repack;
-- from a shell:
pg_repack --table=public.orders --jobs=4 -d app</pre>It copies live rows into a new table, captures concurrent changes via a trigger, and takes a brief exclusive lock only to swap. Needs a PK or unique not-null index and free disk ~ the table's live size + indexes.</li><li><strong>Indexes only</strong>: <code>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> if mainly indexes are bloated.</li><li><strong>Accept some free space</strong>: if the table will grow back into it, plain VACUUM is enough; the goal is a stable size, not a minimal one.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['bloat','maintenance'] },
{ q: `Disk usage spikes by 200 GB within minutes and then drops. What could it be?`,
  a: `<p>Short-lived spikes are usually <strong>temporary files</strong> from a query sorting or hashing far more data than <code>work_mem</code>.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong> from statistics and logs:<pre>SELECT datname, temp_files, pg_size_pretty(temp_bytes) FROM pg_stat_database;
SELECT queryid, calls, temp_blks_written, left(query, 80)
FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY temp_blks_written DESC LIMIT 5;</pre>With <code>log_temp_files = 0</code> each temp file is logged with its statement.</li><li><strong>Typical culprits</strong>: a report with a missing join condition (cartesian product), DISTINCT/ORDER BY over a huge result, a hash join where the planner underestimated the build side, or a CREATE INDEX on a big table.</li><li><strong>Fix the query</strong> (indexes that provide order, correct joins, pre-aggregation) or give that role more memory: <code>ALTER ROLE reporting SET work_mem = '256MB'</code>.</li><li><strong>Contain</strong>: <code>temp_file_limit = '50GB'</code> so one query errors out instead of filling the disk, and place <code>temp_tablespaces</code> on a separate volume if needed.</li><li>Other candidates: WAL bursts from a bulk job (pg_wal), or log files in debug mode.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['disk','temp-files','memory'] },
{ q: `The Linux OOM killer killed a PostgreSQL backend and the whole cluster restarted. What happened and how do you prevent it?`,
  a: `<p>When any backend dies abnormally, the postmaster terminates all others and performs crash recovery, because shared memory may be corrupt. So one OOM kill = full outage of seconds to minutes.</p><ol><li><strong>Find the trigger</strong>: <code>dmesg</code>/journal for "Out of memory: Killed process", and the PostgreSQL log for "terminated by signal 9" with the query that was running.</li><li><strong>Estimate memory demand</strong>: shared_buffers + connections × (per-backend overhead) + concurrent sort/hash nodes × work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier + maintenance_work_mem × autovacuum workers. A few large reports with high work_mem and parallel workers are the classic cause.</li><li><strong>Configure the OS</strong>: <code>vm.overcommit_memory = 2</code> with a suitable <code>vm.overcommit_ratio</code> so allocations fail with an error instead of the OOM killer; protect the postmaster (<code>oom_score_adj = -1000</code> in the systemd unit, with <code>PG_OOM_ADJUST_FILE</code> so backends reset to 0).</li><li><strong>Reduce demand</strong>: lower global work_mem and grant more per role, cap connections via a pooler, limit parallel workers, avoid huge IN-lists and per-session caches (many partitions × many connections bloat catalog caches).</li><li>Use huge pages to shrink page table memory.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['memory','os','incident'] },
{ q: `A dashboard shows total order counts and takes 25 seconds to load. How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Look at the query</strong>: usually <code>SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE ...</code> plus several aggregates over the full history on every page load.</li><li><strong>Decide on precision</strong>: does the business need exact live numbers? If approximate is fine:<pre>SELECT reltuples::bigint AS approx_rows FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'orders'::regclass;</pre></li><li><strong>Precompute</strong>: a summary table per day/status updated incrementally (or by a scheduled job), so the dashboard sums 600 rows instead of 50M:<pre>CREATE TABLE order_daily_stats AS
SELECT order_date::date AS day, status, count(*) AS orders, sum(total_amount) AS revenue
FROM orders GROUP BY 1, 2;</pre>Or a materialized view refreshed <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> every few minutes.</li><li><strong>Index for the remaining live part</strong>: e.g. only today's data computed live with an index on <code>order_date</code>, plus history from the summary.</li><li><strong>Make count scans cheaper</strong>: index-only scans need a well-vacuumed visibility map; parallel query helps large scans.</li><li><strong>Cache</strong> in the application for 30–60 seconds; run heavy dashboards on a replica.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['count','reporting'] },
{ q: `A monthly finance report runs on the primary and makes the OLTP application slow. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm the impact</strong>: during the report, check CPU, I/O wait, <code>pg_stat_activity</code> wait events, and whether it evicts the OLTP working set from shared_buffers.</li><li><strong>Move it off the primary</strong>: run it on a streaming replica (configure <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> or <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> so it is not cancelled) or a dedicated analytics replica.</li><li><strong>Make it cheaper</strong>: <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>; add BRIN or B-tree indexes on date ranges, pre-aggregate daily summaries, avoid repeated scans.</li><li><strong>Isolate resources</strong> if it must stay: a dedicated role with limited <code>work_mem</code>, <code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code>, <code>statement_timeout</code>, and a schedule outside peak hours.</li><li><strong>Long transactions</strong>: a 2-hour report holds a snapshot that blocks vacuum cleanup on the primary — another reason to move it.</li><li><strong>Long term</strong>: CDC or logical replication into a warehouse/columnar store for analytics.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['reporting','replication'] },
{ q: `Queries on a read replica fail with "canceling statement due to conflict with recovery". How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<p>The standby must replay WAL from the primary; some records (VACUUM removing tuples a replica query still needs, exclusive locks from DDL, dropped tablespaces) conflict with running queries. After <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> (default 30s) the standby cancels the query.</p><ol><li><strong>See which conflicts</strong>:<pre>SELECT datname, confl_snapshot, confl_lock, confl_bufferpin, confl_deadlock
FROM pg_stat_database_conflicts;</pre></li><li><strong>Snapshot conflicts</strong> (most common): enable <code>hot_standby_feedback = on</code> so the replica reports its oldest xmin and the primary does not vacuum rows it needs. Cost: bloat on the primary while long replica queries run.</li><li><strong>Or tolerate delay</strong>: raise <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> (e.g. 15min) on a reporting replica — replication lag grows while queries hold up replay. Use separate replicas for HA (low delay) and reporting (high delay).</li><li><strong>Lock conflicts</strong>: DDL or the truncation at the end of VACUUM on the primary; for the latter set <code>vacuum_truncate = false</code> on the affected tables.</li><li><strong>Make the queries shorter</strong> and retry on cancellation (SQLSTATE 40001).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['replication','replica'] },
{ q: `Logical replication to a subscriber stopped; the log shows a duplicate key error on the subscriber. What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>The apply worker hit a conflict (row already exists on the subscriber, often because someone wrote to the subscriber directly or data was loaded twice) and keeps retrying the same transaction. Meanwhile the slot on the publisher retains WAL.</p><ol><li><strong>Check status</strong> on the subscriber:<pre>SELECT subname, worker_type, received_lsn, latest_end_lsn, last_msg_receipt_time
FROM pg_stat_subscription;
SELECT subname, apply_error_count, sync_error_count FROM pg_stat_subscription_stats;</pre></li><li><strong>Read the error</strong>: the log names the relation, the key and the finish LSN of the failing remote transaction.</li><li><strong>Resolve the data</strong>: if the subscriber's row is wrong, delete or fix it on the subscriber so the change can apply; replication resumes automatically.</li><li><strong>Or skip the transaction</strong> (PG 15+) if you accept losing it: <code>ALTER SUBSCRIPTION sub SKIP (lsn = '0/14C0378');</code> — then reconcile manually.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: make subscriber tables read-only for applications, use <code>disable_on_error = true</code> so it stops cleanly and alerts, and monitor publisher slot lag. PG 18 adds conflict statistics columns in <code>pg_stat_subscription_stats</code> to track conflict types.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['logical-replication','incident'] },
{ q: `In a multi-tenant SaaS, one large tenant makes the database slow for everyone. What can you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Attribute load</strong>: include the tenant in <code>application_name</code> or a query comment, or group pg_stat_statements by role if tenants have separate roles; check which tenant's queries dominate time and I/O.</li><li><strong>Fix plan issues caused by skew</strong>: the big tenant has 40% of rows, so generic plans tuned for small tenants are wrong for it — raise statistics on <code>tenant_id</code>, use custom plans, and make sure every index leads with <code>tenant_id</code>.</li><li><strong>Limit blast radius</strong>: per-tenant rate limiting and connection quotas in the app/pooler; <code>statement_timeout</code> for tenant API roles; push heavy exports to a replica.</li><li><strong>Isolate physically</strong>: list partitioning by tenant (the big tenant gets its own partition, others share a default/hash partition), or move the big tenant to its own database/cluster. Distributed options: the Citus <span class="tag ext">extension</span> with tenant isolation.</li><li><strong>Row-level security</strong> keeps isolation correct if you restructure (policies on <code>tenant_id</code>).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['multi-tenant','performance'] },
{ q: `During a flash sale, stock decrements on popular products time out. How do you fix inventory contention?`,
  a: `<p>Every buyer of the same product updates the same row: <code>UPDATE products SET stock = stock - 1 WHERE id = 7</code>. Row locks serialize them, and if the transaction also does payment calls, each holds the lock for hundreds of ms.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: many sessions waiting on <code>Lock: transactionid</code> with the same blocker chain, all on one product.</li><li><strong>Shrink the critical section</strong>: decrement as the last statement and commit immediately; never call external services while holding the lock. A single atomic conditional update avoids read-then-write races:<pre>UPDATE products SET stock = stock - 1
WHERE id = 7 AND stock &gt;= 1
RETURNING stock;   -- 0 rows returned = sold out</pre></li><li><strong>Fail fast</strong>: <code>SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '200ms'</code> and let the app retry or queue.</li><li><strong>Split the hot row</strong>: pre-allocate stock into N bucket rows per product and decrement a random bucket with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, or reserve tickets/units as individual rows claimed with SKIP LOCKED.</li><li><strong>Queue the orders</strong> for extreme sales (accept the request, process sequentially per product, notify the user).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['locks','contention'] },
{ q: `A job queue table that used to dequeue in 1 ms now takes 2 seconds. What's wrong?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</strong> the dequeue query: typically the index scan for <code>status = 'queued' ORDER BY id</code> walks over thousands of dead index entries at the head of the queue before finding a live row.</li><li><strong>Check dead tuples and the xmin horizon</strong>:<pre>SELECT n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname = 'jobs';
SELECT max(age(backend_xmin)) FROM pg_stat_activity;</pre>A long transaction anywhere in the database prevents cleanup of those dead rows.</li><li><strong>Fix the horizon</strong> (kill/fix long transactions), then vacuum: <code>VACUUM jobs;</code></li><li><strong>Tune the table</strong>: <code>ALTER TABLE jobs SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0, autovacuum_vacuum_threshold = 1000, autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay = 0);</code></li><li><strong>Design</strong>: a partial index <code>ON jobs (id) WHERE status = 'queued'</code>; move completed jobs to a history table or delete them; consider partitioning the history by day and dropping old partitions; use <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> for workers.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['queues','vacuum'] },
{ q: `Right after an application deploy, connection count spikes to the max and logins time out. What's happening?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Connection storm</strong>: all new app instances start simultaneously, each opening its full pool (min pool size) at once, while old instances still hold theirs. With TLS + SCRAM authentication each new connection costs CPU, so the server slows, clients time out and retry — amplifying the storm.</li><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: <code>SELECT count(*), state FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;</code> and the log with <code>log_connections = on</code>.</li><li><strong>Immediate</strong>: slow the rollout, terminate idle connections of old instances, temporarily lower pool minimums.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: put <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer between app and DB so client connections are cheap and server connections are capped; set app pool <code>minIdle</code> low and connection creation rate-limited; use rolling deploys with backoff and jitter on retries.</li><li><strong>Guardrails</strong>: per-role <code>CONNECTION LIMIT</code> and reserved connections for admins.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['connections','pooling'] },
{ q: `After introducing PgBouncer in transaction mode, the app gets "prepared statement "S_1" does not exist" errors. Why?`,
  a: `<p>In transaction pooling mode, each transaction may run on a different server connection. The driver prepared the statement on server connection A, and the next transaction executes it on connection B, where it does not exist (or B already has a different <code>S_1</code>: "already exists").</p><ol><li><strong>Upgrade PgBouncer</strong> to 1.21+ and set <code>max_prepared_statements</code> (e.g. 200): PgBouncer then tracks protocol-level prepared statements and transparently prepares them on whichever server connection is used.</li><li><strong>Or disable server-side prepares in the driver</strong>: e.g. <code>prepareThreshold=0</code> for pgJDBC, <code>No Reset On Close</code>/<code>Max Auto Prepare=0</code> for Npgsql, <code>statement_cache_size=0</code> for asyncpg.</li><li><strong>Check other session state</strong> that breaks in transaction mode: <code>SET</code> outside a transaction, session advisory locks, <code>LISTEN</code>, temp tables — use <code>SET LOCAL</code> and transaction-level advisory locks.</li><li>SQL-level <code>PREPARE</code> statements are not handled by PgBouncer's tracking — avoid them through the pooler.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['pgbouncer','connections'] },
{ q: `Every 5 minutes, write latency spikes for about 30 seconds. What do you suspect?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Correlate with checkpoints</strong>: set <code>log_checkpoints = on</code> (default on since PG 15) and compare checkpoint start/complete timestamps with the latency graph. The 5-minute rhythm matches the default <code>checkpoint_timeout</code>.</li><li><strong>Check the checkpoint stats</strong>:<pre>SELECT num_timed, num_requested, write_time, sync_time, buffers_written
FROM pg_stat_checkpointer;   -- pg_stat_bgwriter before PG 17</pre>A long <code>sync_time</code> means the fsync phase floods the device.</li><li><strong>Spread the writes</strong>: <code>checkpoint_completion_target = 0.9</code>, larger <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (15min) and <code>max_wal_size</code> so checkpoints are less frequent and not forced.</li><li><strong>Tune OS writeback</strong> so dirty pages are flushed continuously rather than in bursts: lower <code>vm.dirty_background_bytes</code>; PostgreSQL's <code>checkpoint_flush_after</code>/<code>bgwriter_flush_after</code> help too.</li><li><strong>Also</strong>: full-page writes after each checkpoint increase WAL volume briefly — <code>wal_compression</code> reduces it. Check storage throughput limits (cloud IOPS caps).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['checkpoints','io'] },
{ q: `Deleting a single customer takes 40 seconds. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</strong> the delete inside a transaction you roll back:<pre>BEGIN;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) DELETE FROM customers WHERE id = 42;
ROLLBACK;</pre>The output lists <code>Trigger for constraint orders_customer_id_fkey: time=39800.123 calls=1</code>.</li><li><strong>Cause</strong>: the referencing column <code>orders.customer_id</code> has no index. For every deleted parent row, PostgreSQL runs a query on <code>orders</code> to check (or cascade) references — a full sequential scan of a large table.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>:<pre>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_id_idx ON orders (customer_id);</pre></li><li><strong>Audit all FKs</strong> for missing indexes by comparing <code>pg_constraint.conkey</code> (contype = 'f') with the leading columns of <code>pg_index.indkey</code> on the referencing table.</li><li>Also check for slow user triggers and cascades through several levels of child tables.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['foreign-keys','indexes'] },
{ q: `A query fails with "ERROR: invalid page in block 1234 of relation base/16384/24576". What do you do?`,
  a: `<p>Data corruption: a page failed its header sanity check or checksum. Usual causes are hardware/storage faults, filesystem bugs, or unsafe configuration (<code>fsync = off</code>, write caches without battery).</p><ol><li><strong>Do not panic-write</strong>: avoid running VACUUM FULL or other rewrites before you have a copy. Take a file-level copy of the data directory (or snapshot) for forensics.</li><li><strong>Identify the object</strong>: <code>SELECT pg_filenode_relation(0, 24576);</code> — an index can simply be rebuilt with <code>REINDEX</code>.</li><li><strong>Check scope</strong>: run <code>pg_amcheck</code> (core client tool, PG 14+) across the database to find other damaged relations; check the system logs for disk errors.</li><li><strong>Recover data</strong>: the cleanest path is failover to a healthy replica (if the corruption is local to the primary's storage) or restore from backup + PITR. For a table with only a few bad pages, you can salvage other rows with <code>zero_damaged_pages = on</code> in one session while copying the table out — this destroys the bad page's rows, so use only on a copy.</li><li><strong>Root cause</strong>: replace failing hardware, enable data checksums (<code>initdb --data-checksums</code>, default in PG 18; or <code>pg_checksums --enable</code> offline), and verify backups regularly.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['corruption','incident'] },
{ q: `After bulk-importing data with explicit IDs, inserts fail with "duplicate key value violates unique constraint orders_pkey". Why?`,
  a: `<p>The import inserted IDs directly (e.g. <code>OVERRIDING SYSTEM VALUE</code> or <code>COPY</code> with the id column), but the identity sequence was not advanced, so <code>nextval</code> now returns IDs that already exist.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>:<pre>SELECT max(id) FROM orders;
SELECT last_value FROM orders_id_seq;   -- or pg_get_serial_sequence('orders', 'id')</pre></li><li><strong>Fix</strong> by moving the sequence past the max:<pre>SELECT setval(pg_get_serial_sequence('orders', 'id'),
              (SELECT max(id) FROM orders));</pre>For identity columns you can also use <code>ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN id RESTART WITH 50001;</code></li><li><strong>Check all tables</strong> touched by the import the same way.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: include sequence resets in import scripts (<code>pg_dump</code> does this automatically with <code>setval</code>), and remember logical replication does not replicate sequence values — reset them at cutover.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['sequences','data-import'] },
{ q: `A daily sales report shows different totals for users in India and in the US. What is going on?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Check the column type</strong>: <code>timestamptz</code> stores an absolute instant; it is displayed and cast to <code>date</code> using the session's <code>TimeZone</code>. <code>order_date::date</code> therefore groups by a different calendar day depending on the client's time zone setting.</li><li><strong>Reproduce</strong>:<pre>SET TimeZone = 'Asia/Kolkata';
SELECT order_date::date AS day, count(*) FROM orders GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1 LIMIT 3;
SET TimeZone = 'America/New_York';
SELECT order_date::date AS day, count(*) FROM orders GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1 LIMIT 3;</pre></li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: define the business day explicitly in SQL, independent of the session:<pre>SELECT (order_date AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata')::date AS business_day, sum(total_amount)
FROM orders GROUP BY 1;</pre></li><li><strong>Indexes</strong>: if you filter by business day, filter with timestamptz ranges (<code>order_date &gt;= '2025-10-01 00:00+05:30' AND order_date &lt; '2025-10-02 00:00+05:30'</code>) so an index on <code>order_date</code> is usable.</li><li><strong>Standardize</strong>: store <code>timestamptz</code>, set the server/database default <code>TimeZone</code> deliberately, and convert at the presentation layer.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['timezones','sql'] },
{ q: `A developer ran an UPDATE touching 80M rows; now replicas lag 40 minutes and the table doubled in size. What now?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>If it is still running</strong>: decide whether to cancel. Cancelling rolls back instantly (MVCC marks the transaction aborted), but all the written tuples become dead and still need vacuuming; WAL already generated still has to replay on replicas.</li><li><strong>Replica lag</strong>: replicas are replaying single-threaded; route reads that need fresh data to the primary until <code>replay_lag</code> recovers; do not start other bulk jobs.</li><li><strong>Space</strong>: run <code>VACUUM (VERBOSE) big_table;</code> once committed/aborted so the dead space becomes reusable — the file will not shrink, but it will stop growing as new writes reuse the space. Make sure no long transaction holds the xmin horizon.</li><li><strong>Indexes</strong>: non-HOT updates added entries to every index; check bloat and <code>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> if needed; use <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_repack if the table must shrink.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: batch large updates (10–50k rows per transaction with pauses and lag checks), review mass DML before production, and give developers a role with <code>statement_timeout</code>.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['writes','replication','bloat'] },
{ q: `You partitioned orders by month, but EXPLAIN shows the query scanning all 60 partitions. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Check the predicate</strong>: pruning only works when the query constrains the partition key directly. These do not prune:<pre>WHERE date_trunc('month', order_date) = '2025-06-01'   -- function on key
WHERE order_date::date = '2025-06-15'                 -- cast on key
WHERE customer_id = 42                                  -- key not used at all</pre>Rewrite as a range on the raw key: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2025-06-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-07-01'</code>.</li><li><strong>Type mismatches</strong>: comparing a <code>timestamptz</code> key with a <code>timestamp</code> value requires a time-zone-dependent (stable) conversion, so pruning moves to run time; EXPLAIN ANALYZE should then show <code>Subplans Removed</code>.</li><li><strong>Generic plans</strong>: with prepared statements the plan lists all partitions but prunes at executor startup; verify with EXPLAIN ANALYZE rather than plain EXPLAIN.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong>: confirm <code>enable_partition_pruning = on</code>.</li><li><strong>Design check</strong>: if the dominant queries filter by <code>customer_id</code>, monthly range partitioning does not fit them — consider sub-partitioning or a different key, and make sure each partition has an index on <code>customer_id</code>.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','planner'] },
{ q: `Legal says order data older than 7 years must be removed monthly. How do you implement it efficiently?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Partition by time</strong> (monthly range on <code>order_date</code>) so retention is a metadata operation instead of a mass DELETE.</li><li><strong>Monthly job</strong>:<pre>ALTER TABLE orders DETACH PARTITION orders_2018_09 CONCURRENTLY;  -- PG 14+, no blocking
-- optional: archive first
-- COPY orders_2018_09 TO PROGRAM 'gzip &gt; /archive/orders_2018_09.csv.gz' WITH (FORMAT csv);
DROP TABLE orders_2018_09;</pre><code>DETACH ... CONCURRENTLY</code> cannot run in a transaction block and requires that there is no default partition.</li><li><strong>Create future partitions ahead</strong> (a few months), via a scheduled job or the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_partman, so inserts never fail for lack of a partition.</li><li><strong>Child tables with FKs</strong>: <code>order_items</code> and <code>payments</code> referencing orders need the same partitioning scheme (and partition key in the FK) or must be purged first.</li><li><strong>Migration of an existing big table</strong>: create a new partitioned table, attach the old table as one big historical partition (with a CHECK constraint matching the bound to avoid a validation scan), and let it age out.</li><li><strong>Compliance</strong>: also purge backups/archives per policy and document it.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','retention'] },
{ q: `pg_stat_statements shows one query consuming 40% of total database time. What's your process?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Characterize it</strong>:<pre>SELECT calls, total_exec_time, mean_exec_time, stddev_exec_time, rows,
       shared_blks_hit, shared_blks_read, temp_blks_written, query
FROM pg_stat_statements WHERE queryid = 123456789;</pre>High calls + low mean = frequency problem (N+1, missing cache); low calls + high mean = plan problem; high stddev = parameter-dependent plans.</li><li><strong>Get real parameters</strong> from application logs or <code>auto_explain</code>, and run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> with representative and worst-case values.</li><li><strong>Fix the plan</strong>: index matching filters and sort, covering index for index-only scans, rewrite (EXISTS instead of IN with DISTINCT, keyset pagination, avoid functions on columns), statistics fixes.</li><li><strong>Fix the frequency</strong>: batch lookups, cache results in the application, remove redundant calls.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong>: reset or snapshot stats and compare total time over a comparable window; confirm no new query took its place.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['pg_stat_statements','methodology'] },
{ q: `After switching to SERIALIZABLE isolation, the app sees many "could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies" errors. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Understand the contract</strong>: SERIALIZABLE (Serializable Snapshot Isolation) guarantees results equal to some serial order by aborting transactions that could violate it. Failures with SQLSTATE <code>40001</code> are expected and the application <strong>must retry</strong> the whole transaction.</li><li><strong>Add retries</strong> with backoff around each transaction (idempotent logic, recompute inside the retry).</li><li><strong>Reduce false positives</strong>: SSI tracks predicate locks; without indexes it locks whole relations/pages, causing more conflicts. Index the columns used in WHERE clauses; keep transactions short; mark read-only transactions <code>READ ONLY</code> (and <code>DEFERRABLE</code> for long reports).</li><li><strong>Tune predicate lock memory</strong> if escalation to coarser locks is high: <code>max_pred_locks_per_transaction</code>, <code>max_pred_locks_per_relation</code>.</li><li><strong>Reconsider scope</strong>: maybe only a few workflows need SERIALIZABLE; others can use READ COMMITTED with explicit row locks or constraints.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['transactions','isolation'] },
{ q: `Two requests create the same user simultaneously; one fails with a unique violation or you get duplicates. How do you make it race-free?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Root cause</strong>: a check-then-insert pattern (<code>SELECT ... ; if not found INSERT</code>) is not atomic under READ COMMITTED.</li><li><strong>Enforce uniqueness in the database</strong>: a unique constraint or unique index on the natural key (e.g. <code>lower(email)</code>).</li><li><strong>Use an atomic upsert</strong>:<pre>INSERT INTO users (username, email, password_hash, role, tenant_id)
VALUES ('asha', 'asha@example.com', 'x', 'viewer', 1)
ON CONFLICT (email) DO UPDATE SET last_login = now()
RETURNING id;</pre><code>ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code> returns no row on conflict, so re-select if you need the id.</li><li><strong>MERGE</strong> (PG 15+) is not race-free in the same way; under concurrency it can still raise unique violations — prefer <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT</code> for upserts.</li><li><strong>Retry</strong> on <code>23505</code> as a last resort for complex flows, or serialize with <code>pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext(email))</code>.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['concurrency','upsert'] },
{ q: `You need to add a UNIQUE constraint on email, but the table already contains duplicates. How do you do it safely?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Find duplicates</strong>:<pre>SELECT lower(email) AS email_norm, count(*), array_agg(id ORDER BY id) AS ids
FROM customers GROUP BY 1 HAVING count(*) &gt; 1;</pre></li><li><strong>Decide merge rules with the business</strong>: which record survives, how orders and other references are re-pointed.</li><li><strong>Stop new duplicates first</strong> if possible (application check), then clean up in a transaction: re-point child rows, delete losers.<pre>WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY lower(email) ORDER BY id) AS rn
  FROM customers)
SELECT id FROM ranked WHERE rn &gt; 1;   -- ids to merge away</pre></li><li><strong>Create the index without blocking</strong>, then attach it as a constraint:<pre>CREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY customers_email_lower_uq ON customers (lower(email));
-- a plain-column unique index can become a constraint:
-- ALTER TABLE customers ADD CONSTRAINT customers_email_uq UNIQUE USING INDEX customers_email_uq;</pre>If new duplicates sneak in during the build, it fails and leaves an INVALID index — drop and retry.</li><li><strong>Normalize going forward</strong>: store emails lowercased, or use the citext <span class="tag ext">extension</span> / a nondeterministic collation.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['constraints','data-quality'] },
{ q: `Users complain the product search box (name contains term) takes 3 seconds. How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Look at the query</strong>: usually <code>WHERE name ILIKE '%term%'</code> or <code>lower(name) LIKE '%term%'</code> — a full sequential scan.</li><li><strong>Substring search</strong>: trigram GIN index via the contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_trgm:<pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_name_trgm ON products USING gin (name gin_trgm_ops);
SELECT id, name FROM products WHERE name ILIKE '%usb c%' LIMIT 20;</pre></li><li><strong>Word search with relevance</strong>: core full-text search with a generated <code>tsvector</code> column and GIN index, <code>websearch_to_tsquery</code> for user input and <code>ts_rank</code> for ordering.</li><li><strong>Typos and fuzzy matching</strong>: trigram similarity (<code>name % 'headphnes'</code> ordered by <code>similarity()</code>).</li><li><strong>UX</strong>: minimum 3 characters, debounce keystrokes, LIMIT results, and cancel superseded requests.</li><li>Beyond this (facets, synonyms, multi-language analyzers at scale), consider a dedicated search engine, fed via CDC.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['search','pg_trgm'] },
{ q: `Queries filtering on a JSONB attributes column are slow. How do you speed them up?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Check the operators used</strong>: a GIN index only helps containment/existence/jsonpath operators, not <code>attributes-&gt;&gt;'brand' = 'Acme'</code>.</li><li><strong>Containment queries</strong>: rewrite to <code>@&gt;</code> and add a GIN index:<pre>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_attrs_gin ON products USING gin (attributes jsonb_path_ops);
SELECT id FROM products WHERE attributes @&gt; '{"brand": "Acme"}';</pre></li><li><strong>One hot key</strong>: a B-tree expression index is smaller and supports ranges and sorting:<pre>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_brand ON products ((attributes-&gt;&gt;'brand'));
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_weight ON products (((attributes-&gt;'specs'-&gt;&gt;'weight_kg')::numeric));</pre></li><li><strong>Statistics</strong>: the planner has no per-key statistics on JSONB without an expression index (or extended statistics on the expression), so estimates may be wild.</li><li><strong>Model check</strong>: keys that every row has and every query filters on belong in real columns (typed, constrained, smaller, better statistics).</li><li><strong>TOAST</strong>: large documents are detoasted on every access — select only the keys you need.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['jsonb','indexes'] },
{ q: `Semantic search with pgvector got slow (or results got worse) as the table grew to 20M embeddings. What do you check?`,
  a: `<p>pgvector is an <span class="tag ext">extension</span>, not core PostgreSQL.</p><ol><li><strong>Is an ANN index used?</strong> EXPLAIN should show an index scan on an HNSW or IVFFlat index; without one every query is an exact sequential scan. The operator in ORDER BY must match the index opclass (<code>&lt;=&gt;</code> with <code>vector_cosine_ops</code>, <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> with <code>vector_l2_ops</code>), with a LIMIT.</li><li><strong>Filtered search</strong>: a WHERE filter applied after the ANN scan can return too few rows. pgvector 0.8+ offers iterative scans (<code>SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order</code>); also consider partial indexes per tenant/category or partitioning.</li><li><strong>Recall vs speed</strong>: HNSW <code>SET hnsw.ef_search = 100</code> (default 40) improves recall at higher cost; IVFFlat <code>ivfflat.probes</code>; IVFFlat lists built on early data drift as data grows — rebuild.</li><li><strong>Memory</strong>: HNSW performs best when the index fits in RAM; check index size versus shared_buffers/OS cache; consider <code>halfvec</code> (half precision) or fewer dimensions.</li><li><strong>Builds</strong>: raise <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> so the HNSW graph builds in memory, and use parallel builds.</li></ol><pre>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY docs_embedding_hnsw ON doc_chunks
  USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops) WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);</pre>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','ai'] },
{ q: `An API exports all orders page by page using OFFSET and becomes unusably slow after page 5,000. Fix it.`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Diagnose</strong>: EXPLAIN ANALYZE on a deep page shows the scan producing OFFSET + LIMIT rows and discarding most: cost grows linearly with page number, and the full export becomes quadratic.</li><li><strong>Switch to keyset pagination</strong> using the last seen key as a cursor token:<pre>SELECT id, customer_id, order_date, total_amount
FROM orders
WHERE id &gt; $1          -- last id from the previous page (0 for the first)
ORDER BY id
LIMIT 1000;</pre>Every page is an index range scan on the primary key — constant time.</li><li><strong>Stable results</strong>: keyset also avoids duplicated/skipped rows when data changes between pages.</li><li><strong>For bulk exports</strong>, consider a server-side cursor in one transaction or <code>COPY (SELECT ...) TO STDOUT</code> streamed to the client, run against a replica.</li><li><strong>API design</strong>: return an opaque <code>next_cursor</code> instead of page numbers.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['pagination','api'] },
{ q: `After a major version upgrade with pg_upgrade, everything is slow for the first hour. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Missing statistics</strong>: before PG 18, pg_upgrade did not transfer optimizer statistics, so the planner works with defaults until ANALYZE runs — bad join choices everywhere. (PG 18 transfers most statistics, but not extended statistics.)</li><li><strong>Fix immediately</strong>:<pre>vacuumdb --all --analyze-in-stages --jobs=8</pre>It produces minimal statistics quickly, then progressively better ones.</li><li><strong>Run extended statistics ANALYZE</strong> as well on PG 18 (a plain <code>ANALYZE</code> or <code>vacuumdb --analyze-only --missing-stats-only</code>).</li><li><strong>Cold cache</strong>: shared_buffers and OS cache start empty; the contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_prewarm can warm key tables and indexes.</li><li><strong>Visibility map/hint bits</strong>: first reads set hint bits causing writes; a <code>VACUUM</code> helps.</li><li><strong>Next time</strong>: include the analyze step in the downtime runbook before opening traffic and benchmark on a restored copy first.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['upgrade','statistics'] },
{ q: `Database credentials for the application were leaked in a public repository. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Rotate immediately</strong>: <code>ALTER ROLE app PASSWORD 'new-strong-secret';</code> (SCRAM-SHA-256 with <code>password_encryption = 'scram-sha-256'</code>), deploy the new secret to the app via a secret manager. Existing sessions stay connected — terminate the role's sessions after redeploy:<pre>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE usename = 'app' AND backend_start &lt; now() - interval '5 minutes';</pre></li><li><strong>Check for abuse</strong>: connection logs (<code>log_connections</code>), unusual client addresses in <code>pg_stat_activity</code>, the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgaudit logs if enabled, and data changes in audit tables.</li><li><strong>Limit exposure</strong>: <code>pg_hba.conf</code> should only allow app subnets with <code>hostssl</code> and scram; the database should not be reachable from the internet.</li><li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: the app role should not own tables or be superuser; verify with <code>\\du</code> in psql and grants.</li><li><strong>Clean the repository</strong> history and add secret scanning; move to short-lived credentials (IAM auth, Vault dynamic secrets) where possible.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','incident'] },
{ q: `Someone ran DROP TABLE payments in production. How do you get it back?`,
  a: `<p>DROP TABLE removes the files at commit; there is no recycle bin.</p><ol><li><strong>Contain</strong>: stop application paths that write to payments-related tables or now fail; find the exact time and the transaction from logs (<code>log_statement = 'ddl'</code> is a must-have).</li><li><strong>Delayed replica</strong> (if you have one with <code>recovery_min_apply_delay</code>): pause replay immediately (<code>SELECT pg_wal_replay_pause();</code>) — the table still exists there; copy it out.</li><li><strong>PITR</strong> otherwise: restore a base backup to a separate host with <code>recovery_target_time</code> just before the DROP (or <code>recovery_target_xid</code>), pause, and verify the table.</li><li><strong>Copy back</strong>: <code>pg_dump -t payments</code> from the recovered instance and restore into production, including indexes, constraints, grants and dependent objects (views dropped by CASCADE!). Then merge rows written to the recovered source after the target, if any.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: production roles for apps and humans must not own tables (only owners can drop); use an event trigger to block DROP outside migrations; require reviewed migrations.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['backup','pitr','security'] },
{ q: `Analysts need read-only SQL access to production data. How do you set it up safely?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Route them to a replica</strong>, not the primary.</li><li><strong>Create a group role with least privilege</strong>:<pre>CREATE ROLE analysts NOLOGIN;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE app TO analysts;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO analysts;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO analysts;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE app_owner IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO analysts;
CREATE ROLE asha LOGIN PASSWORD '...' IN ROLE analysts;</pre>Or use the predefined role <code>pg_read_all_data</code> (PG 14+) if they may read everything.</li><li><strong>Hide sensitive columns</strong>: revoke table SELECT and grant column-level SELECT, or expose views without PII (e.g. no <code>password_hash</code>, masked emails); RLS for row restrictions.</li><li><strong>Guardrails</strong>: <code>ALTER ROLE analysts SET statement_timeout = '5min'</code>, <code>default_transaction_read_only = on</code>, <code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code>, connection limits, and <code>work_mem</code> per role.</li><li><strong>Audit</strong>: log their statements (<code>log_statement</code> per role or the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgaudit) and review access periodically.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','roles'] },
{ q: `After a crash, PostgreSQL is taking 25 minutes to start. What's happening?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Crash recovery</strong> is replaying WAL from the last checkpoint's redo point. The log shows "database system was interrupted; last known up at..." and "redo starts at ...". PG 15+ logs progress periodically (<code>log_startup_progress_interval</code>).</li><li><strong>Why so long</strong>: a very large <code>max_wal_size</code> / <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> means lots of WAL to replay; replay is single-threaded and random-I/O bound on cold cache.</li><li><strong>Do not interrupt it</strong> or delete WAL; let it finish. Check disk throughput and that <code>pg_wal</code> is on fast storage.</li><li><strong>If HA is available</strong>, failing over to a replica is usually faster than waiting.</li><li><strong>Tune trade-off</strong>: shorter checkpoint intervals reduce recovery time but increase WAL and I/O in normal operation — pick based on your RTO. <code>recovery_prefetch</code> (PG 15+, default try) speeds replay by prefetching referenced blocks.</li><li><strong>Find the crash cause</strong> afterwards (OOM kill, segfault in an extension, storage failure).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['recovery','checkpoints'] },
{ q: `A standby logs "requested WAL segment 0000000100000A2B000000F1 has already been removed". What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Meaning</strong>: the standby was disconnected or too slow, and the primary recycled WAL it still needed. Without a replication slot, retention is only <code>wal_keep_size</code>.</li><li><strong>Recover from the archive</strong> if one exists: configure <code>restore_command</code> on the standby so it fetches missing segments from the WAL archive, then streams again once caught up.</li><li><strong>Otherwise rebuild</strong> the standby:<pre>pg_basebackup -h primary -D /var/lib/postgresql/data -U replicator \\
  -X stream -C -S standby1_slot -R -P</pre>(<code>-C -S</code> creates a physical slot, <code>-R</code> writes the standby configuration.) For very large databases, <code>pg_rewind</code> is not applicable here; use a backup tool restore (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest delta restore) to save time.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: use replication slots with <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> as a safety cap, configure <code>restore_command</code> fallback to the archive, and alert on replication lag.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['replication','wal'] },
{ q: `The data volume (not WAL) is at 98% and growing. What do you do right now and afterwards?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Buy time</strong>: expand the volume if possible (cloud volumes grow online); otherwise free non-database space (old logs, core dumps, stale backups on the same disk). Never delete files inside the data directory manually.</li><li><strong>Find what grew</strong>:<pre>SELECT c.oid::regclass AS relation, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(c.oid)) AS size
FROM pg_class c
WHERE c.relkind IN ('r','m')
ORDER BY pg_total_relation_size(c.oid) DESC LIMIT 15;</pre>Compare with yesterday's sizes (keep a history table!). Also check temp files and <code>pg_wal</code>.</li><li><strong>Quick wins</strong>: drop unused indexes (<code>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>), drop or detach old partitions, truncate staging/log tables that can be emptied. Avoid <code>VACUUM FULL</code> — it needs extra space to rewrite.</li><li><strong>Bloat</strong>: if a table is bloated, vacuum it so space is reused (stops growth), then repack later when space allows.</li><li><strong>Afterwards</strong>: alert at 70/80/90%, capacity forecast from size history, retention policies, and separate volumes for WAL and temp.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['disk','incident'] },
{ q: `VACUUM FULL on a large table failed halfway with "could not extend file: No space left on device". What now?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Good news</strong>: VACUUM FULL writes a new copy and swaps only at the end; on failure the transaction aborts and the original table is intact. The partially written new file is removed at abort.</li><li><strong>Confirm space came back</strong> (<code>df</code>) and check the database is healthy (no other failures from the full disk, e.g. WAL writes).</li><li><strong>Plan the rewrite properly</strong>: VACUUM FULL needs free space roughly equal to the table's live data plus all its indexes, and holds an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock the whole time.</li><li><strong>Alternatives</strong>: <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_repack (also needs space, but no long lock); repack one large index at a time with <code>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>; or if the table is partitioned, rewrite partition by partition.</li><li><strong>Often unnecessary</strong>: if the goal is to stop growth, a plain VACUUM makes dead space reusable without extra disk.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','disk'] },
{ q: `pg_cancel_backend() on a runaway query returns true, but the query keeps running. What next?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cancel is cooperative</strong>: the backend checks for interrupts at safe points. Long loops in some C extensions, waits in uninterruptible system calls (network file system, stuck I/O) or a query waiting on a client socket may delay it.</li><li><strong>Escalate to terminate</strong>: <code>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(12345);</code> ends the whole session (its transaction is rolled back). PG 14+ accepts a timeout: <code>pg_terminate_backend(12345, 5000)</code> waits up to 5 s for confirmation.</li><li><strong>Check what it is doing</strong>: <code>wait_event</code> in <code>pg_stat_activity</code>, OS tools (<code>strace -p</code>, <code>/proc/PID/stack</code>).</li><li><strong>Never <code>kill -9</code></strong> a backend: the postmaster treats it as a crash and restarts all connections with crash recovery. A plain <code>kill</code> (SIGTERM) equals pg_terminate_backend.</li><li><strong>Rollback time</strong>: terminating a huge write transaction is fast (MVCC abort), but if it was a big <code>CREATE INDEX</code> or similar, cleanup of files happens at abort.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['operations','sessions'] },
{ q: `An ALTER TABLE is stuck behind "autovacuum: VACUUM public.orders (to prevent wraparound)". What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Understand</strong>: normal autovacuum yields to conflicting lock requests (it cancels itself), but an <strong>anti-wraparound</strong> autovacuum does not. Its lock (SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE) blocks DDL like ALTER TABLE, and your waiting DDL now blocks all queries behind it.</li><li><strong>Unblock traffic first</strong>: cancel your ALTER (it is the one causing the queue), and reschedule the migration.</li><li><strong>Let the vacuum finish</strong>: killing it only restarts it later from scratch, and wraparound protection is non-negotiable. Speed it up if possible (it uses <code>autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit</code>; changing it with reload affects running workers). Watch <code>pg_stat_progress_vacuum</code>.</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: freeze proactively during quiet hours (<code>VACUUM (FREEZE) orders</code> before relfrozenxid age hits <code>autovacuum_freeze_max_age</code>), keep autovacuum fast enough that freeze vacuums are cheap (visibility/frozen map), and always use <code>lock_timeout</code> for DDL.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','locks','migrations'] },
{ q: `The same query sometimes takes 5 ms and sometimes 8 seconds. How do you investigate?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Collect samples of slow runs</strong> with plans: <code>auto_explain</code> (contrib) with <code>log_min_duration = '1s'</code>, <code>log_analyze</code>, <code>log_buffers</code>. Compare slow and fast plans and their parameter values.</li><li><strong>Different plans</strong>: parameter-dependent selectivity (one huge customer) or a switch to a <strong>generic plan</strong> after five executions of a prepared statement. Test:<pre>PREPARE q(int) AS SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 10;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) EXECUTE q(42);
SET plan_cache_mode = force_generic_plan;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) EXECUTE q(42);</pre></li><li><strong>Same plan, different time</strong>: then it is the environment — lock waits (<code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code>), cold cache (<code>shared read</code> vs hit), I/O stalls during checkpoints, CPU saturation, or JIT compile time.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong> accordingly: an index that serves all parameter values well (e.g. composite <code>(customer_id, order_date DESC)</code>), <code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code> for the role, shorter conflicting transactions, or checkpoint smoothing.</li><li><strong>Measure p95/p99</strong>, not averages, and track them per queryid over time.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['planner','latency'] },
{ q: `The database server shows high I/O wait and slow queries. How do you narrow it down?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>OS level</strong>: <code>iostat -x 1</code> — device utilization, await (latency), read vs write IOPS and throughput; is it the data volume or the WAL volume?</li><li><strong>Who does the I/O</strong> (PG 16+):<pre>SELECT backend_type, object, context, reads, read_time, writes, write_time
FROM pg_stat_io ORDER BY reads + writes DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10;</pre>Enable <code>track_io_timing</code> to get times.</li><li><strong>Which queries</strong>: pg_stat_statements <span class="tag ext">extension</span> ordered by <code>shared_blks_read</code> (and <code>shared_blk_read_time</code>).</li><li><strong>Classify</strong>: reads from sequential scans of large tables (missing index, working set larger than RAM), writes from checkpoints, autovacuum (throttle via cost settings), temp files (work_mem), or a backup/replica rebuild reading the disk.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: indexes and query fixes to reduce reads; more RAM; checkpoint tuning; move WAL to its own device; faster storage or more provisioned IOPS.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['io','monitoring'] },
{ q: `On a cloud VM, the database is fast for hours, then suddenly every query becomes 10x slower. What could it be?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Burst credits</strong>: many cloud volume types (and small instance types) give burst IOPS/CPU credits; once exhausted, performance drops to the baseline. Check the cloud metrics (burst balance, IOPS consumed vs provisioned, CPU credit balance).</li><li><strong>Correlate</strong> with PostgreSQL: rising <code>IO: DataFileRead</code> wait events and read latency in <code>pg_stat_io</code>, with no change in workload.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: provisioned-IOPS volumes or larger volumes (baseline scales with size), non-burstable instances, more RAM to reduce reads.</li><li><strong>Reduce I/O</strong>: indexes for top read queries, autovacuum and checkpoint tuning to smooth writes, avoid heavy batch jobs during peak.</li><li>Other periodic slowdowns to rule out: backups, noisy neighbours, scheduled VACUUM/REINDEX jobs, storage snapshots.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['cloud','io'] },
{ q: `Finance requires invoice numbers without gaps, but sequences produce gaps. How do you implement it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Why sequences gap</strong>: <code>nextval</code> is never rolled back (so concurrent transactions do not block each other), and values cached per session or lost on crash also leave gaps. Sequences are the right tool for IDs, not for gapless numbers.</li><li><strong>Gapless counter table</strong> updated in the same transaction as the invoice — serializes invoice creation per series, which is acceptable for invoicing rates:<pre>CREATE TABLE invoice_counters (series text PRIMARY KEY, last_no bigint NOT NULL);
-- inside the invoice transaction:
UPDATE invoice_counters SET last_no = last_no + 1
WHERE series = 'IN-2025' RETURNING last_no;</pre>If the transaction rolls back, the increment rolls back too.</li><li><strong>Keep the transaction short</strong>: assign the number as the last step just before commit.</li><li><strong>Alternative</strong>: assign numbers asynchronously in a single job after the invoice is final.</li><li><strong>Confirm the requirement</strong>: many jurisdictions require sequential, non-duplicated numbering with documented reasons for voided numbers rather than strictly gapless; cancellations should create credit notes, not deletions.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['sequences','design'] },
{ q: `After an OS upgrade, PostgreSQL logs "collation version mismatch" warnings and some unique lookups miss rows. What happened?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cause</strong>: text indexes are sorted by the collation rules of the C library (glibc) or ICU. Upgrading glibc (notably 2.28) changed sort order for many strings, so existing B-tree indexes on text are no longer correctly ordered — lookups can miss rows and unique indexes can admit duplicates.</li><li><strong>Find affected databases and indexes</strong>:<pre>SELECT datname, datcollversion, pg_database_collation_actual_version(oid)
FROM pg_database;</pre>Index candidates are those on text/varchar columns using non-C collations; the contrib <code>amcheck</code> <span class="tag ext">extension</span> (<code>bt_index_check</code>) or <code>pg_amcheck</code> detects ordering violations.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: rebuild affected indexes (<code>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY ...</code> or <code>REINDEX DATABASE</code>), resolve any duplicates found, then record the new version: <code>ALTER DATABASE app REFRESH COLLATION VERSION;</code></li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: treat OS/glibc upgrades like PostgreSQL upgrades (logical replication or reindex plan), or use the C / builtin (PG 17+) collation provider for keys that do not need linguistic sorting, or ICU with versioned collations.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['collation','indexes','upgrade'] },
{ q: `An ORM generates queries with IN lists of 20,000 IDs and they are slow. What can you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure where the time goes</strong>: EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows Planning Time; huge literal lists make parsing/planning expensive and each list length is a separate entry in pg_stat_statements.</li><li><strong>Pass an array parameter</strong> instead:<pre>SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = ANY($1::bigint[]);</pre>One prepared statement, one parameter, efficient index scan with array keys (improved in PG 17).</li><li><strong>For very large sets, join</strong>: <code>JOIN unnest($1::bigint[]) AS k(id) USING (id)</code> or a temp table that you <code>ANALYZE</code>, letting the planner choose a hash join.</li><li><strong>Question the pattern</strong>: why does the app fetch 20k ids? Often it first selects IDs then fetches rows — do it in one query with a join or subquery.</li><li><strong>Driver limits</strong>: the protocol allows at most 65535 bind parameters per statement — another reason to use one array parameter.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['application','sql'] },
{ q: `Users update their profile and immediately see the old value. Reads go to a replica. How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cause</strong>: asynchronous replication — the replica applies the change milliseconds to seconds later, so a read right after the write returns stale data (read-your-writes violation).</li><li><strong>Session stickiness</strong>: after a user writes, route that user's reads to the primary for a few seconds (store a timestamp in the session/cookie).</li><li><strong>LSN-based consistency</strong>: after the write, capture <code>SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn();</code>; before reading on a replica, check <code>SELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() &gt;= '0/3000148'::pg_lsn;</code> and fall back to the primary or wait briefly.</li><li><strong>synchronous_commit = remote_apply</strong> with a synchronous standby makes commits wait until the standby has applied — consistent reads there, but higher write latency and availability coupling.</li><li><strong>Return the new value from the write</strong> (<code>UPDATE ... RETURNING</code>) so the UI does not need an immediate re-read.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['replication','consistency'] },
{ q: `Inserts into the orders table became 5x slower after someone added an audit trigger. How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)</code> of an INSERT (in a rolled-back transaction) shows <code>Trigger audit_orders: time=... calls=...</code>; <code>pg_stat_user_functions</code> (with <code>track_functions = 'pl'</code>) shows trigger function time.</li><li><strong>Common problems</strong>: <code>FOR EACH ROW</code> trigger doing several queries per row, building jsonb with <code>to_jsonb(NEW)</code> plus diffing, writing to an audit table with many indexes, or a missing index used by a lookup inside the trigger.</li><li><strong>Fix options</strong>:<ul><li>Use statement-level triggers with transition tables for bulk operations:<pre>CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit AFTER INSERT ON orders
REFERENCING NEW TABLE AS new_rows
FOR EACH STATEMENT EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_orders_stmt();</pre></li><li>Minimize audit table indexes; partition it by time.</li><li>Use <code>WHEN</code> conditions so the trigger fires only when relevant columns change.</li><li>Move auditing out of the write path via logical decoding / CDC.</li></ul></li></ol>`,
  tags: ['triggers','writes'] },
{ q: `You need to add a foreign key from order_items (2B rows) to products without blocking writes. How?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Plain ADD CONSTRAINT</strong> validates every existing row while holding a SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE lock on both tables — writes blocked for the duration of a huge scan.</li><li><strong>Two-step approach</strong>:<pre>SET lock_timeout = '3s';
ALTER TABLE order_items
  ADD CONSTRAINT order_items_product_fk FOREIGN KEY (product_id)
  REFERENCES products (id) NOT VALID;           -- quick: only new rows checked

ALTER TABLE order_items VALIDATE CONSTRAINT order_items_product_fk;
-- scans existing rows with SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE on order_items: reads and writes continue</pre></li><li><strong>Fix orphans first</strong>: if validation fails, find them with an anti-join (<code>NOT EXISTS</code>) and clean them up.</li><li><strong>Index the referencing column</strong> (<code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ... (product_id)</code>) so deletes/updates on products stay fast.</li><li>The same pattern works for CHECK constraints (<code>NOT VALID</code> + <code>VALIDATE</code>).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['migrations','constraints'] },
{ q: `The log volume filled up because someone set log_min_duration_statement = 0. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Immediate</strong>: reset it and reload — no restart needed:<pre>ALTER SYSTEM RESET log_min_duration_statement;   -- or edit postgresql.conf
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
SELECT name, setting, source FROM pg_settings WHERE name = 'log_min_duration_statement';</pre>Also check role/database-level overrides (<code>ALTER ROLE ... SET</code>).</li><li><strong>Free space</strong>: compress or remove old log files; if the logs share the data volume, this is urgent — a full data volume stops the database.</li><li><strong>Right-size logging</strong>: <code>log_min_duration_statement = '500ms'</code> (or sampling with <code>log_min_duration_sample</code> + <code>log_statement_sample_rate</code>), <code>log_rotation_age</code>/<code>log_rotation_size</code>, and external rotation/shipping.</li><li><strong>Separate volumes</strong> for logs, and alerts on their usage. Use pg_stat_statements (<span class="tag ext">extension</span>) for full query statistics instead of logging every statement.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['logging','disk'] },
{ q: `Nightly pg_dump backups of a 2 TB database take 10 hours and sometimes overlap business hours. What do you change?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>pg_dump is a logical export</strong>, good for migrations and single-table restores, poor as the main backup for large databases: slow to take, very slow to restore (indexes rebuilt), no point-in-time recovery, and it holds a long snapshot that blocks vacuum cleanup.</li><li><strong>Switch to physical backups + WAL archiving</strong>: <code>pg_basebackup</code> (core; PG 17+ supports incremental backups with <code>--incremental</code> and <code>pg_combinebackup</code>) or <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest/Barman with parallel, compressed, incremental/differential backups and PITR.</li><li><strong>Back up from a replica</strong> to keep load off the primary.</li><li><strong>If you keep pg_dump</strong>: directory format with parallel jobs (<code>pg_dump -Fd -j 8</code>), run it on a replica, exclude large regenerable tables.</li><li><strong>Test restores</strong> and measure RTO with the new approach.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['backup'] },
{ q: `After an unplanned failover, users report that orders placed seconds before the outage are missing. Why, and how do you prevent it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cause</strong>: with asynchronous replication (the default), the primary confirms COMMIT once WAL is flushed locally. Transactions not yet streamed to the replica are lost when it is promoted. The gap equals the replication lag at the moment of failure.</li><li><strong>Assess</strong>: if the old primary's disk is accessible, the lost transactions are in its WAL; you can extract them (start it isolated and diff rows by timestamp, or decode WAL) and re-apply carefully.</li><li><strong>Prevent with synchronous replication</strong>:<pre>synchronous_standby_names = 'ANY 1 (replica1, replica2)'
synchronous_commit = on</pre>COMMIT waits until at least one standby flushed the WAL. Use two or more sync candidates so one standby failure does not stop writes.</li><li><strong>Selective durability</strong>: keep sync for critical tables' transactions and use <code>SET LOCAL synchronous_commit = local</code> for low-value writes.</li><li><strong>HA manager</strong>: Patroni (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>) can enforce <code>synchronous_mode</code> and only fail over to a synchronous standby; <code>maximum_lag_on_failover</code> limits data loss in async mode.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['ha','replication','durability'] },
{ q: `A customer saw another tenant's data due to a missing WHERE tenant_id filter in one query. How do you prevent this class of bug?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Immediate</strong>: fix and deploy the query, assess the exposure (logs), and follow your incident/notification process.</li><li><strong>Enforce isolation in the database with Row-Level Security</strong>:<pre>ALTER TABLE users ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE users FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON users
  USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::int)
  WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::int);</pre>The app sets the tenant per transaction: <code>SET LOCAL app.tenant_id = '2'</code> (or <code>set_config('app.tenant_id', '2', true)</code>), which also works with PgBouncer transaction pooling.</li><li><strong>The app role must not bypass RLS</strong>: not superuser, not table owner (or FORCE RLS), no <code>BYPASSRLS</code>.</li><li><strong>Performance</strong>: lead indexes with <code>tenant_id</code>; keep the policy expression simple so it is applied as an index condition.</li><li><strong>Test</strong>: automated tests that query as tenant A and assert no tenant B rows; fail closed when the setting is missing (<code>current_setting(..., true)</code> returns NULL → no rows).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','rls','multi-tenant'] },
{ q: `Rows keep "mysteriously disappearing" from a table. How do you find out what deletes them?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Check cascades</strong>: does this table reference a parent with <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>? Deleting parent rows silently deletes the children:<pre>SELECT conname, conrelid::regclass, confrelid::regclass, confdeltype
FROM pg_constraint WHERE contype = 'f' AND conrelid = 'order_items'::regclass;</pre>(<code>confdeltype = 'c'</code> means cascade.)</li><li><strong>Check triggers and rules</strong> on the table and its parents; check scheduled jobs (pg_cron <span class="tag ext">extension</span>, OS cron) and retention scripts.</li><li><strong>Statistics</strong>: <code>n_tup_del</code> in <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code> increasing confirms real deletes (vs. data never inserted or filtered by RLS).</li><li><strong>Capture who</strong>: temporarily log DML for the table — an audit trigger writing <code>current_user</code>, <code>inet_client_addr()</code>, <code>application_name</code> and the old row into <code>audit_logs</code>, or <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgaudit object logging.</li><li><strong>Rule out visibility issues</strong>: RLS policies, wrong search_path (different schema with the same table name), or reading from a lagging replica.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['troubleshooting','audit'] },
{ q: `After adding a new index, a previously fast query became slow because the planner picked the new index. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Compare plans</strong> with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS): the planner thinks the new index is cheaper (e.g. it matches the ORDER BY so it can stop early with LIMIT) but actual rows show it scans far more than estimated.</li><li><strong>Fix the estimate</strong> where possible: ANALYZE, higher statistics target, extended statistics on correlated columns.</li><li><strong>Test without the index</strong> safely in a transaction (DROP INDEX inside BEGIN blocks the table — do this on a replica or staging copy), or compare with <code>SET enable_indexscan = off</code> in a session for diagnosis only.</li><li><strong>Better index</strong>: usually a composite index that serves both filter and sort (<code>(customer_id, order_date DESC)</code>) beats both alternatives and removes the dilemma.</li><li><strong>If the new index is not needed</strong>, drop it with <code>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>. Rolling out indexes with before/after plan checks for top queries avoids such surprises.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['indexes','planner'] },
{ q: `After a crash and restart, autovacuum seems to have stopped processing several big tables. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cumulative statistics are reset after a crash</strong>: since PG 15 they live in shared memory and are written to disk only on clean shutdown; after crash recovery all counters (<code>n_dead_tup</code>, <code>n_mod_since_analyze</code>, <code>n_ins_since_vacuum</code>) start from zero.</li><li><strong>Effect</strong>: autovacuum decides based on these counters, so it thinks tables have no dead tuples until enough new changes accumulate — bloat and stale statistics grow meanwhile. (Anti-wraparound vacuum still works; it uses <code>relfrozenxid</code>.)</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: run <code>VACUUM (ANALYZE)</code> on the busiest tables (or <code>vacuumdb --all --analyze --jobs=4</code>) after any crash.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong> with <code>pg_stat_user_tables.last_autovacuum</code>/<code>last_vacuum</code>.</li><li>Also check <code>pg_stat_database.stats_reset</code> before trusting "unused index" or similar analyses after a crash.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['autovacuum','statistics'] },
{ q: `An UPDATE ... FROM that syncs prices from a staging table of 2M rows runs for an hour. How do you speed it up?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>EXPLAIN</strong> (without ANALYZE first): look for a nested loop with sequential scans, or a join on unindexed/mismatched-type columns. ANALYZE the staging table — freshly loaded tables have no statistics.</li><li><strong>Index the join key</strong> on the staging table or let it hash join; make sure types match (text vs bigint casts kill indexes).</li><li><strong>Avoid no-op updates</strong> — they still create new row versions:<pre>UPDATE products p
SET price = s.price
FROM staging_prices s
WHERE p.sku = s.sku
  AND p.price IS DISTINCT FROM s.price;</pre></li><li><strong>Duplicate keys in staging</strong>: if staging has duplicates per sku, rows update multiple times unpredictably — dedupe first.</li><li><strong>Batch</strong> for very large updates (by key range) to limit lock duration, WAL bursts and replica lag; vacuum between batches.</li><li><strong>Trigger and index overhead</strong>: disable non-essential triggers during the sync (carefully), and avoid updating indexed columns unnecessarily (HOT).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['writes','sql'] },
{ q: `After adding three indexes for a new feature, WAL volume doubled and replicas started lagging. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure</strong>: <code>wal_bytes</code> per query in pg_stat_statements (<span class="tag ext">extension</span>), <code>pg_stat_wal</code>, and HOT ratio in <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code> before/after.</li><li><strong>Cause</strong>: each index adds WAL for every insert and every non-HOT update; if a new index covers a frequently updated column (e.g. <code>status</code>, <code>updated_at</code>), it also destroys HOT updates, so <em>every</em> index gets new entries on every update.</li><li><strong>Evaluate each index</strong>: is it used (<code>idx_scan</code>)? Could it be partial (<code>WHERE status = 'pending'</code>) or merged with an existing composite index? Drop the ones that do not pay for themselves.</li><li><strong>Restore HOT</strong>: avoid indexing hot-updated columns, and lower fillfactor on the table.</li><li><strong>Reduce WAL</strong>: <code>wal_compression = lz4</code>, longer checkpoint intervals (fewer full-page images).</li><li><strong>Replica side</strong>: confirm replica storage can sustain the new apply rate.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['wal','indexes','replication'] },
{ q: `Black Friday is in four weeks and you expect 10x normal traffic. How do you prepare the database?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Baseline</strong>: current peak QPS, CPU, I/O, connections, replication lag, top queries by total time (pg_stat_statements <span class="tag ext">extension</span>).</li><li><strong>Load test</strong> with production-like data and the expected traffic mix (pgbench custom scripts or app-level load tests) to find the first bottleneck.</li><li><strong>Optimize the top 10 queries</strong> — usually the biggest win: indexes, removing N+1s, caching hot reads (catalog, prices) in the application.</li><li><strong>Connections</strong>: PgBouncer (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>) in transaction mode, sized pools, timeouts.</li><li><strong>Scale reads</strong>: replicas for read traffic, with lag-aware routing; scale the primary vertically ahead of time (resize takes downtime/failover).</li><li><strong>Hot spots</strong>: inventory counters and queues — design with SKIP LOCKED, sharded counters, short transactions.</li><li><strong>Operational freeze</strong>: no migrations or big batch jobs during the event; autovacuum and checkpoints tuned; disk headroom; backups verified; failover tested.</li><li><strong>Runbooks and dashboards</strong> ready, with clear owners and escalation.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['capacity','planning'] },
{ q: `The log warns "database must be vacuumed before N more MultiXactIds are used". What are MultiXacts and what do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>What they are</strong>: when several transactions lock the same row (e.g. <code>FOR SHARE</code>, or FK checks taking <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code> on a parent row from many concurrent child inserts), the row's xmax stores a MultiXactId pointing to a list of members in <code>pg_multixact</code>. Like XIDs they are 32-bit and must be frozen before wraparound; the members space can also fill up.</li><li><strong>Check ages</strong>:<pre>SELECT datname, mxid_age(datminmxid) FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC;
SELECT oid::regclass, mxid_age(relminmxid) FROM pg_class
WHERE relkind = 'r' ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10;</pre></li><li><strong>Vacuum the oldest tables</strong> (<code>VACUUM (FREEZE)</code>), after removing anything holding back the horizon (long transactions, old prepared transactions).</li><li><strong>Tune</strong>: <code>autovacuum_multixact_freeze_max_age</code> and per-table settings so freezing happens earlier.</li><li><strong>Reduce generation</strong>: very hot parent rows referenced by many concurrent FK inserts are the classic source; consider batching child inserts or reducing explicit <code>FOR SHARE</code> locks.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','wraparound','locks'] },
{ q: `You must move a 2 TB production database to a new server (or cloud) with under 5 minutes of downtime. How?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Choose the method</strong>: physical streaming replication if the target runs the same major version and platform; <strong>logical replication</strong> if versions differ or you move to a managed service.</li><li><strong>Logical replication plan</strong>:<pre>-- source (wal_level = logical)
CREATE PUBLICATION migrate_pub FOR ALL TABLES;
-- target: schema first (pg_dump --schema-only | psql), then
CREATE SUBSCRIPTION migrate_sub
  CONNECTION 'host=old-db dbname=app user=replicator'
  PUBLICATION migrate_pub;</pre>Every table needs a primary key or <code>REPLICA IDENTITY</code>; DDL, sequences and large objects are not replicated.</li><li><strong>Monitor catch-up</strong>: initial table sync state in <code>pg_subscription_rel</code>, lag via the slot's <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> on the source.</li><li><strong>Validate</strong>: row counts and checksums of key tables, test queries on the target, run ANALYZE.</li><li><strong>Cutover</strong>: stop writes (app maintenance mode or revoke), wait for lag zero, copy sequence values (<code>setval</code>), switch connection strings/DNS, re-enable writes. Keep the old server for rollback (optionally reverse replication).</li><li><strong>Rehearse</strong> the cutover on a copy and time each step.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['migration','logical-replication'] },
{ q: `You need to rename a column that a live application uses, without downtime. How?`,
  a: `<p><code>ALTER TABLE ... RENAME COLUMN</code> is instant, but old application instances still use the old name during a rolling deploy and will fail. Use <strong>expand/contract</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>Expand</strong>: add the new column; keep both in sync with a trigger (or write both from the app); backfill in batches.</li><li><strong>Deploy app version that reads the new column</strong> and writes both.</li><li><strong>Deploy app version that uses only the new column</strong> once all instances are updated.</li><li><strong>Contract</strong>: drop the trigger and the old column (<code>ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN</code> is metadata-only; space is reclaimed by later rewrites).</li><li><strong>Simpler alternative</strong> for read-heavy cases: rename the column and create a view with the old name (via an updatable view or a separate schema on the search_path) for the transition period.</li><li>Always use <code>lock_timeout</code> for the DDL steps.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['migrations','zero-downtime'] },
{ q: `Auditors require a record of who changed what in the payments table. How do you implement it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Clarify requirements</strong>: which operations, old/new values, retention, tamper resistance, and whether reads must be audited too.</li><li><strong>Row-change history with a trigger</strong> (works on any PostgreSQL):<pre>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $fn$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_logs (table_name, operation, row_id, old_data, new_data, changed_by, changed_at)
  VALUES (TG_TABLE_NAME, TG_OP,
          COALESCE((to_jsonb(NEW) -&gt;&gt; 'id'), (to_jsonb(OLD) -&gt;&gt; 'id'))::bigint,
          CASE WHEN TG_OP &lt;&gt; 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
          CASE WHEN TG_OP &lt;&gt; 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END,
          current_user, now());
  RETURN NULL;
END $fn$;
CREATE TRIGGER payments_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON payments
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();</pre></li><li><strong>Application identity</strong>: the DB user is usually the shared app role; pass the end user via <code>SET LOCAL app.user_id = '...'</code> and record <code>current_setting('app.user_id', true)</code>.</li><li><strong>Statement/session auditing</strong>: the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgaudit logs DDL, reads and writes to the server log for compliance.</li><li><strong>Protect the trail</strong>: audit table owned by a separate role, apps only have INSERT via the SECURITY DEFINER trigger; ship logs off-host; partition the audit table by month.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','audit'] },
{ q: `A user exercises their right to erasure (GDPR). How do you delete their data across the system?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Map personal data</strong>: all tables and columns holding PII for a customer (customers, orders addresses, audit_logs JSON, search indexes, logs, caches, analytics copies, backups).</li><li><strong>Decide delete vs anonymize</strong>: financial records may need to be kept for legal reasons — anonymize the person (name, email, phone) but keep the transaction amounts.<pre>BEGIN;
UPDATE customers
SET name = 'deleted user', email = 'deleted+' || id || '@invalid', city = NULL
WHERE id = 42;
UPDATE audit_logs
SET old_data = old_data - 'email' - 'name', new_data = new_data - 'email' - 'name'
WHERE table_name = 'customers' AND row_id = 42;
COMMIT;</pre></li><li><strong>Physical removal</strong>: old row versions remain in heap pages until VACUUM, and in WAL and backups until they expire — document backup retention as part of the policy.</li><li><strong>Replicas and downstream systems</strong>: logical subscribers, warehouses and search engines must receive the deletion too.</li><li><strong>Design for it</strong>: keep PII in few tables keyed by customer id; consider per-customer encryption keys (crypto-shredding).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','compliance'] },
{ q: `A table with 8,000 partitions has queries with 300 ms planning time and "LWLock: LockManager" waits. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows Planning Time dominating; wait-event sampling shows <code>LWLock: LockManager</code>. Each query locks every partition it plans for (and their indexes); beyond the per-backend fast-path slots, locks go to the shared lock table, which becomes a contention point.</li><li><strong>Ensure plan-time pruning</strong>: constants on the partition key let the planner skip most partitions; generic plans with parameters must lock all partitions at plan time (run-time pruning happens later).</li><li><strong>Reduce partition count</strong>: monthly instead of daily, or fewer hash partitions; merge old partitions by detaching them, creating one partition for the combined range and moving the rows (core PostgreSQL 16–18 has no MERGE PARTITIONS command).</li><li><strong>Upgrade</strong>: PG 18 scales fast-path lock slots with <code>max_locks_per_transaction</code>, relieving lock manager contention for queries touching many relations.</li><li><strong>Fewer indexes per partition</strong> and custom plans (<code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code>) for queries where plan-time pruning matters.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','locks','planner'] },
{ q: `The CDC pipeline (Debezium on a logical replication slot) lags hours behind and pg_wal is growing. What do you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure the slot</strong>:<pre>SELECT slot_name, active, confirmed_flush_lsn,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), confirmed_flush_lsn)) AS behind
FROM pg_replication_slots WHERE slot_type = 'logical';</pre>Also check <code>pg_stat_replication_slots</code> (spill_bytes, stream_bytes).</li><li><strong>Decoding cost</strong>: large transactions are spilled to disk by the WAL sender when they exceed <code>logical_decoding_work_mem</code> (64MB); raise it, and enable streaming of in-progress transactions in the consumer if supported.</li><li><strong>Consumer bottleneck</strong>: Debezium (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>) / Kafka Connect throughput, sink backpressure, or the connector acknowledging offsets rarely — the slot only advances when the consumer confirms.</li><li><strong>Reduce the stream</strong>: publish only needed tables (<code>CREATE PUBLICATION ... FOR TABLE</code>, row filters and column lists in PG 15+), avoid massive batch updates on published tables.</li><li><strong>Protect the primary</strong>: <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> so a stuck consumer cannot fill the disk (the slot becomes invalid and the pipeline needs a resnapshot), and alerts on slot lag.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['logical-replication','cdc','wal'] },
{ q: `The database server is using swap and latency is erratic. What do you check?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Confirm</strong>: <code>free -m</code>, <code>vmstat 1</code> (si/so columns), and which processes are swapped.</li><li><strong>Memory budget</strong>: shared_buffers (too large?), connections × per-backend memory (catalog caches grow with many tables/partitions; long-lived connections accumulate), work_mem × concurrent nodes, maintenance memory × autovacuum workers, plus other software on the host.</li><li><strong>Immediate</strong>: reduce connection count (pooler), lower work_mem for heavy roles, recycle long-lived connections (pool max lifetime), stop non-DB processes.</li><li><strong>OS settings</strong>: <code>vm.swappiness = 1</code> on a dedicated DB host; huge pages for shared_buffers (cannot be swapped and reduce page table memory); disable Transparent Huge Pages.</li><li><strong>Right-size</strong>: if the working set and connections genuinely need more memory, scale RAM.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['memory','os'] },
{ q: `Each API request opens a new database connection and connection setup takes 30–80 ms. How do you fix it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Measure</strong>: <code>log_connections</code>/<code>log_disconnections</code> show session durations; very short sessions with high counts confirm per-request connections.</li><li><strong>Cost of a new connection</strong>: TCP + TLS handshake, SCRAM authentication round trips, fork of a backend process, and cold catalog caches for the first queries.</li><li><strong>Use a connection pool</strong> in the application (HikariCP, Npgsql built-in pooling, pgx pool, SQLAlchemy pool) so connections are reused.</li><li><strong>Serverless/short-lived processes</strong> (Lambda, PHP-FPM) cannot pool locally: use an external pooler — <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer or a managed proxy (RDS Proxy).</li><li><strong>Network</strong>: keep the app and database in the same region/zone; TLS session resumption helps where supported.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['connections','pooling','latency'] },
{ q: `Someone set statement_timeout = '30s' in postgresql.conf; now nightly maintenance and backups fail. What's the better approach?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Problem</strong>: a global timeout applies to every session — pg_dump (which sets its own statement_timeout to 0 in recent versions, but other tools may not), <code>CREATE INDEX</code>, VACUUM jobs, migrations and reports.</li><li><strong>Move it to roles</strong>:<pre>ALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = '30s';
ALTER ROLE reporting SET statement_timeout = '15min';
ALTER ROLE maintenance SET statement_timeout = 0;</pre>and reset the global setting (<code>pg_reload_conf()</code> after editing).</li><li><strong>Per transaction overrides</strong>: <code>SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '5min'</code> for a known long operation.</li><li><strong>Check precedence</strong>: <code>SELECT name, setting, source FROM pg_settings WHERE name = 'statement_timeout';</code> — session &gt; role-in-database &gt; role &gt; database &gt; config file.</li><li>Pair with <code>lock_timeout</code> and <code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code> per role for a consistent guardrail policy.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['gucs','operations'] },
{ q: `pg_attribute and pg_class have grown to several GB and every query's planning got slower. What's going on?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Catalog bloat</strong>: every <code>CREATE TEMP TABLE</code> inserts rows into <code>pg_class</code>, <code>pg_attribute</code> (one per column), <code>pg_type</code> and more; dropping them leaves dead catalog tuples. An application creating thousands of temp tables per minute bloats the catalogs.</li><li><strong>Confirm</strong>:<pre>SELECT relname, n_dead_tup, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(oid::regclass)) AS size
FROM pg_stat_sys_tables
WHERE relname IN ('pg_attribute', 'pg_class', 'pg_type', 'pg_depend');</pre></li><li><strong>Why vacuum doesn't keep up</strong>: long transactions or a logical slot's <code>catalog_xmin</code> hold back catalog cleanup.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: remove the horizon blocker, then <code>VACUUM</code> the catalogs; for severe bloat, <code>VACUUM FULL pg_attribute</code> in a maintenance window (takes exclusive locks on a catalog — everything waits).</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: reuse temp tables per session (<code>ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS</code>), use CTEs/arrays/unnest instead of temp tables, or regular tables with a session key.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['catalog','bloat','vacuum'] },
{ q: `A migration fails with "ERROR: out of shared memory HINT: You might need to increase max_locks_per_transaction". Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Cause</strong>: the shared lock table has room for about <code>max_locks_per_transaction × (max_connections + max_prepared_transactions)</code> object locks. One transaction that touches thousands of relations — dropping a schema with many tables, querying a table with thousands of partitions (each with indexes), or <code>pg_dump</code> of a huge schema — can exhaust it.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: raise <code>max_locks_per_transaction</code> (default 64) to e.g. 256–1024; it requires a restart and uses a little more shared memory. On standbys it must be at least as large as on the primary.</li><li><strong>Or split the work</strong>: drop/alter objects in smaller transactions.</li><li><strong>Partition-heavy workloads</strong>: fewer partitions or ensure pruning so queries lock fewer relations.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['locks','gucs'] },
{ q: `The pg_largeobject table is 500 GB, but the application says it deleted most files. Why didn't space come back?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Large objects are not deleted with the row</strong> that references them: deleting a row with an <code>oid</code> column leaves the large object orphaned unless the application calls <code>lo_unlink</code> or a trigger does.</li><li><strong>Find orphans</strong> with the contrib client tool <code>vacuumlo</code> (dry run with <code>-n</code>), which removes large objects not referenced by any oid/lo column:<pre>vacuumlo -n -v app        # report
vacuumlo -v app           # remove orphans</pre></li><li><strong>Reclaim space</strong>: VACUUM <code>pg_largeobject</code> to make space reusable; returning it to the OS needs a rewrite (VACUUM FULL with a lock, in a window).</li><li><strong>Prevent</strong>: the contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> lo provides a <code>lo_manage</code> trigger that unlinks objects automatically; better yet, store files in object storage (S3) and keep only metadata/URLs in PostgreSQL, or use <code>bytea</code> for small files.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['storage','bloat'] },
{ q: `After an ORM upgrade, a lookup by uuid became a sequential scan. Nothing changed in the database. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Compare the SQL and parameter types</strong>: log the statement (<code>log_min_duration_statement</code> or auto_explain) — the new ORM version sends the parameter as <code>text</code> or <code>varchar</code> instead of <code>uuid</code>.</li><li><strong>Effect</strong>: depending on the operator chosen, the column side gets cast (<code>id::text = $1</code>), which cannot use the uuid index. The same happens with <code>numeric</code> parameters against integer columns or <code>timestamp</code> vs <code>timestamptz</code>.</li><li><strong>Reproduce</strong>:<pre>EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id::text = '42';   -- seq scan
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = '42';          -- index scan (literal coerced to bigint)</pre></li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: configure the ORM/driver type mapping (e.g. set parameter type explicitly), or cast the parameter, not the column: <code>WHERE id = $1::uuid</code>.</li><li><strong>Catch early</strong>: run EXPLAIN-based regression checks on top queries in CI after dependency upgrades.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['application','indexes'] },
{ q: `Management wants a disaster-recovery drill: prove you can run from the DR region. How do you do it safely?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Define targets</strong>: RPO (acceptable data loss) and RTO (time to recover) agreed with the business.</li><li><strong>Non-disruptive option</strong>: restore the latest backup + WAL in the DR region to a new instance and run validation queries and a smoke test of the application against it — proves backups, not failover.</li><li><strong>Full switchover drill</strong> (planned window): stop writes, wait for the DR standby to catch up (<code>replay_lsn</code> equals the primary's), promote it (<code>pg_promote()</code> or <code>patronictl switchover</code> with the <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> Patroni), point applications to it, run for a period, then switch back the same way, rebuilding the old primary as a standby (<code>pg_rewind</code>).</li><li><strong>Measure</strong> each step's duration and any data discrepancy; check DNS TTLs, secrets, firewall rules and monitoring in the DR region — these usually fail, not PostgreSQL.</li><li><strong>Document gaps</strong> and repeat quarterly.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['dr','ha'] },
{ q: `A large table needs to move to a faster disk (new tablespace) without downtime. How?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>ALTER TABLE ... SET TABLESPACE</strong> copies the files while holding an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock — fine for small tables, downtime for big ones.</li><li><strong>Indexes can move with low impact</strong> by rebuilding them there: PG 14+ supports <code>REINDEX (TABLESPACE fast_ssd, CONCURRENTLY) INDEX orders_pkey</code>, or create a new index concurrently in the new tablespace (<code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ... TABLESPACE fast_ssd</code>) and drop the old one.</li><li><strong>Tables</strong>: the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_repack supports <code>--tablespace</code> (and <code>--moveidx</code>) to rebuild online into another tablespace.</li><li><strong>Partitioned tables</strong>: create new partitions in the new tablespace (<code>ALTER TABLE parent SET TABLESPACE</code> affects future partitions) and move hot partitions one at a time.</li><li><strong>Alternatively</strong>, migrate the whole cluster to faster storage via a replica built on the new disks and a switchover.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['storage','operations'] },
{ q: `VACUUM on a 2 TB table has been running for three days. What can you do?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>See the phase</strong>:<pre>SELECT pid, relid::regclass, phase, heap_blks_total, heap_blks_scanned,
       heap_blks_vacuumed, index_vacuum_count, max_dead_tuple_bytes, dead_tuple_bytes
FROM pg_stat_progress_vacuum;   -- PG 17 column names</pre>A high <code>index_vacuum_count</code> means memory for dead tuples filled repeatedly, so all indexes were scanned many times.</li><li><strong>Throttling</strong>: autovacuum's cost limit may be the bottleneck; raise <code>autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit</code> (reload applies to running workers) or run a manual VACUUM (unthrottled by default) with high <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>.</li><li><strong>Many indexes</strong>: each pass scans every index; drop unused ones; manual <code>VACUUM (PARALLEL 4)</code> vacuums indexes in parallel. PG 17+ dead tuple storage is far more compact.</li><li><strong>Emergency (wraparound)</strong>: <code>VACUUM (INDEX_CLEANUP OFF)</code> freezes the heap without index passes; the PG 14+ failsafe does this automatically near wraparound.</li><li><strong>Long term</strong>: partition the table so vacuum works on smaller pieces and old partitions stay frozen; vacuum more often so each run is small.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','maintenance'] },
{ q: `A security review demands encryption in transit and at rest for PostgreSQL. What do you implement?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>In transit (core)</strong>: <code>ssl = on</code> with a certificate; in <code>pg_hba.conf</code> use <code>hostssl</code> lines only (reject plain <code>host</code>); clients use <code>sslmode=verify-full</code> to verify the server certificate and hostname. Optionally client certificates (<code>cert</code> auth) for service accounts. Replication connections need TLS too.</li><li><strong>Check</strong>:<pre>SELECT pid, ssl, version, cipher, client_dn
FROM pg_stat_ssl JOIN pg_stat_activity USING (pid);</pre></li><li><strong>At rest</strong>: core PostgreSQL has no built-in transparent data encryption. Use encrypted volumes (LUKS, cloud KMS-encrypted disks) for data, WAL and temp files; encrypt backups (<span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest supports repository encryption). Some vendor distributions offer TDE.</li><li><strong>Column-level</strong>: the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgcrypto for selected sensitive fields, with keys managed outside the database (application-side encryption is usually stronger).</li><li><strong>Passwords</strong>: <code>password_encryption = 'scram-sha-256'</code>, no md5 (deprecated in PG 18).</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','encryption'] },
{ q: `A 15-table reporting query gets a different plan on each run, sometimes 2 s, sometimes 2 min. Why?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>GEQO</strong>: with 12 or more relations (<code>geqo_threshold</code>), PostgreSQL uses the Genetic Query Optimizer, a randomized search — plans can vary between runs (the seed is fixed via <code>geqo_seed</code>, but small changes in statistics change the outcome).</li><li><strong>Collapse limits</strong>: with more than 8 relations (<code>join_collapse_limit</code>, <code>from_collapse_limit</code>), part of the join order follows the query text.</li><li><strong>Options</strong>:<pre>SET geqo_threshold = 20;          -- exhaustive search; more planning time
SET join_collapse_limit = 16;
SET from_collapse_limit = 16;</pre>in the reporting session, then check planning time.</li><li><strong>Restructure</strong>: pre-aggregate large fact tables in a CTE marked <code>MATERIALIZED</code> to bound the problem, or fix the join order explicitly with <code>join_collapse_limit = 1</code> and a carefully written join order.</li><li><strong>Statistics</strong>: big estimate errors in early joins compound; fix them with ANALYZE and extended statistics.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['planner','reporting'] },
{ q: `The primary is at its limits (CPU and IOPS) despite tuning. How do you decide between scaling up, read replicas and sharding?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Characterize the load</strong>: read/write ratio, top queries by total time, data size vs RAM, growth rate.</li><li><strong>Optimization first</strong>: indexes, query fixes, caching, connection pooling, partitioning for maintenance — often a 2–10× gain for little cost.</li><li><strong>Scale up</strong>: more cores, RAM and faster storage are the simplest path; single PostgreSQL nodes handle tens of TB and very high TPS on modern hardware. Limits: cost curve, single-node write ceiling, failover time.</li><li><strong>Read replicas</strong> if reads dominate: route reporting and read-only API traffic to replicas, handling replication lag (read-your-writes).</li><li><strong>Offload</strong>: analytics to a warehouse via CDC, caching of hot reads, queues for async work.</li><li><strong>Sharding</strong> when writes or data size exceed one node: application-level sharding by tenant, or the Citus <span class="tag ext">extension</span> (distributed tables, not native partitioning). Costs: cross-shard queries and transactions, rebalancing, operational complexity — choose a shard key that keeps most queries on one shard.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['scaling','architecture'] },
{ q: `A developer asks for a full copy of the production database to debug an issue locally. How do you handle it?`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Default answer: not raw production data</strong>. Copies on laptops leak PII and break compliance (GDPR, PCI). Ask what they actually need: the schema, a specific customer's data shape, data volume for performance testing, or the failing query.</li><li><strong>Schema and statistics only</strong>: <code>pg_dump --schema-only</code> plus the query and its <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> output from production is often enough for performance bugs.</li><li><strong>Masked subset</strong>: build a sanitized copy in a controlled environment — restore a backup into an isolated staging server, then anonymize:<pre>UPDATE customers
SET name  = 'Customer ' || id,
    email = 'user' || id || '@example.invalid';
UPDATE users SET password_hash = 'x', email = 'user' || id || '@example.invalid';</pre>(or use masking tools such as the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> PostgreSQL Anonymizer), and take subsets that keep referential integrity.</li><li><strong>Synthetic data</strong> at production scale with <code>generate_series</code> for load testing.</li><li><strong>If real data is unavoidable</strong> (rare), grant time-limited, audited, read-only access to a secured replica or staging server instead of handing out a dump.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['security','data-masking'] },
];
